---------------------------------\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nHi, I'm Yash Sharma.\r\n\r\nI completed my Master of Computer Applications from Lovely Professional University.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nMy primary focus is backend development, and\r\n\r\nI've worked with technologies including\r\n\r\n&#x09;\\*\\*Python,\r\n\r\n&#x09;Node.js,\r\n\r\n&#x09;Express.js,\r\n\r\n&#x09;REST APIs,\r\n\r\n&#x09;SQL,\r\n\r\n&#x09;PostgreSQL and\r\n\r\n&#x09;MongoDB\\*\\*.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nDuring my Machine Learning internship,\r\n\r\nI worked extensively with \\*\\*Python\\*\\*,\r\n\r\nwhere I built a modular,\r\n\r\n&#x09;config-driven data and\r\n\r\n&#x09;ML pipeline involving\r\n\r\n&#x09;data ingestion,\r\n\r\n&#x09;preprocessing,\r\n\r\n&#x09;feature engineering and\r\n\r\n&#x09;clustering.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nI also worked on optimizing the\r\n\r\n&#x09;preprocessing pipeline\r\n\r\n&#x09;for better memory efficiency.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nAlongside that,\r\n\r\n&#x09;I've been focusing on\r\n\r\n&#x09;backend development and\r\n\r\n&#x09;have built projects involving \\*\\*REST APIs,\r\n\r\n&#x09;authentication and\r\n\r\n&#x09;authorization,\r\n\r\n&#x09;database operations,\r\n\r\n&#x09;file handling and\r\n\r\n&#x09;deployment\\*\\*.\r\n\r\nMy projects have primarily used\r\n\r\n&#x09;Node.js,\r\n\r\n&#x09;Express.js and\r\n\r\n&#x09;databases such as\r\n\r\n&#x09;PostgreSQL and\r\n\r\n&#x09;MongoDB.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nI'm also comfortable with\r\n\r\n&#x09;\\*\\*SQL and\r\n\r\n&#x09;database concepts\\*\\*, and\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nI'm currently focused on\r\n\r\n&#x09;strengthening my understanding of\r\n\r\n&#x09;\r\n\r\n&#x09;APIs,\r\n\r\n&#x09;databases,\r\n\r\n&#x09;scalability and\r\n\r\n&#x09;performance.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nI'm particularly interested in this opportunity because\r\n\r\nit gives me a chance to combine my\r\n\r\n&#x09;\\*\\*Python experience with\r\n\r\n&#x09;my backend and\r\n\r\n&#x09;database experience\\*\\* and\r\n\r\n&#x09;work on production-oriented software systems.\r\n\r\n	{"size": 6739, "mimetype": "text/markdown"}	pending	\N	2026-08-26 18:33:40.391719+05:30	2026-08-26 18:33:40.391719+05:30
2	streams.md	Backend/Node/streams.md	md	# Node.js Streams\n\n## What Are Streams?\n\nStreams are one of the fundamental concepts in Node.js. They allow reading or writing data piece by piece (chunk by chunk) without loading the entire content into memory.\n\n## Four Types\n\n1. **Readable** — source of data (e.g., `fs.createReadStream`)\n2. **Writable** — destination for data (e.g., `fs.createWriteStream`)\n3. **Duplex** — both readable and writable (e.g., TCP socket)\n4. **Transform** — duplex stream that modifies data (e.g., `zlib.createGzip`)\n\n## Piping\n\nThe `.pipe()` method connects a readable stream to a writable stream:\n\n```js\nfs.createReadStream('input.txt')\n  .pipe(zlib.createGzip())\n  .pipe(fs.createWriteStream('input.txt.gz'));\n```\n\n## Backpressure\n\nWhen the writable stream cannot consume data as fast as the readable produces it, Node.js applies **backpressure** — it pauses the readable stream until the writable stream catches up.\n\n## Why Streams Matter\n\n- Memory efficient: process large files without loading them entirely\n- Time efficient: start processing data before the entire input is available\n- Composable: chain transforms via `.pipe()`\n	{"size": 1130, "source": "cli-ingest", "mtimeMs": 1787746096371.3115}	pending	\N	2026-08-26 18:15:39.75021+05:30	2026-08-27 01:08:00.773317+05:30
