10	dario.md	testing wiki folder/dario.md	md	Dario Amodei (born 1983) is an American artificial intelligence (AI) researcher and entrepreneur. In 2021, he and his sister Daniela Amodei co-founded Anthropic, the company behind the large language model series Claude.[2] Prior to that, he was the vice president of research at OpenAI.[3][4]\r\n\r\nIn his capacity as Anthropic's CEO, Amodei often writes on the benefits and risks of advanced AI systems.[5] He is a proponent of an "entente" strategy in which a coalition of democratic nations use advanced AI systems in military applications to achieve a decisive advantage over adversaries while sharing the benefits with cooperating nations.[6][7][8]\r\n\r\nEarly life and education\r\nDario Amodei was born in San Francisco, California, in 1983.[1] His sister, Daniela, was born four years later.[1] His father, Riccardo Amodei, an Italian-American leather craftsman from Massa Marittima, Tuscany, died when Dario was 23 years old.[9][10] His mother, Elena Engel, a Jewish American born in Chicago, worked as a project manager[11] for libraries.[1][12]\r\n\r\nDario grew up in San Francisco and graduated from Lowell High School.[13] He was a member of the USA Physics Olympiad team in 2000.[14] Amodei began college at Caltech, where he worked with Tom Tombrello as one of his Physics 11 students. He later transferred to Stanford University, where he received a bachelor's degree in physics.[15] He was awarded the Hertz Foundation Fellowship in 2007 while earning his doctorate in biophysics from Princeton University. He later received the Hertz Thesis Prize in 2011 for his doctoral research on electrophysiology of neural circuits.[16] He was a postdoctoral scholar at the Stanford University School of Medicine.[17]\r\n\r\nCareer\r\nFrom November 2014 until October 2015, he worked at Baidu.[18] After that, he worked at Google.[19] In 2016, Amodei joined OpenAI.[20]\r\n\r\nIn 2021, Dario and his sister, Daniela, founded Anthropic along with other former senior members of OpenAI.[21][22] The Amodei siblings were among those who left OpenAI due to directional differences.[23]\r\n\r\nIn November 2023, the board of directors of OpenAI approached Amodei about replacing Sam Altman and potentially merging the two startups. Amodei declined both offers.[24]\r\n\r\nTime magazine listed Amodei as one of the world's 100 most influential people in 2025,[25] and again in 2026 alongside his sister Daniela.[26] He also was named as one of the "Architects of AI" for Time's Person of the Year.[27]\r\n\r\nAs of May 2026, Anthropic has an estimated value of $965 billion,[28] with Forbes estimating Amodei's net worth to be $15.5 billion.[29]\r\n\r\nViews\r\nBenefits of AI\r\nIn October 2024, Amodei published an essay titled "Machines of Loving Grace", in which he speculated about how AI could improve human welfare.[7] In it, he writes, "I think that most people are underestimating just how radical the upside of AI could be, just as I think most people are underestimating how bad the risks could be."[6] The essay described a vision of civilization where the risks of AI had been addressed and powerful AI was applied to raise the quality of life for everyone, suggesting that AI could contribute to enormous advances in biology, neuroscience, economic development, global peace, work, and meaning of lives.[6]\r\n\r\nIn the article, Amodei also stresses the importance "that democracies have the upper hand on the world stage when powerful AI is created", and argues for an "entente" strategy where a coalition of democracies use AI to achieve a decisive strategic and military advantage over their adversaries, while distributing the benefits to all cooperating democratic nations.[6]\r\n\r\nRisks of AI\r\nIn January 2026, Amodei published a follow-up essay titled "The Adolescence of Technology", which focuses on the risks posed by powerful AI.[30][31] In it, he discusses five major categories of AI risk: autonomous systems acting against human interests, misuse for mass destruction, misuse to seize political power, economic disruption, and unforeseen effects of rapid technological change.[32]\r\n\r\nThe first category concerns the possibility that AI systems develop goals or behaviors misaligned with human intentions. He notes that such behaviors have already been observed in testing at Anthropic, including AI models engaging in deception, blackmail, and scheming.[30][33]\r\n\r\nThe second category involves misuse of AI for destruction by individuals or small groups, with Amodei expressing particular concern about biological weapons. He warns that AI could enable people without specialized training to create weapons of mass destruction.[30][33]\r\n\r\nThe third category concerns misuse of AI by powerful actors to seize or maintain power. Amodei cautions that AI could enable authoritarian governments to conduct unprecedented surveillance, deploy autonomous weapons, and engage in mass propaganda. He identifies the Chinese Communist Party as the greatest threat in this regard, arguing that democracies must maintain AI leadership to prevent a "global totalitarian dictatorship".[34]\r\n\r\nThe fourth category addresses economic disruption, including mass labor displacement and concentration of wealth. Amodei notes that AI could displace half of all entry-level white-collar jobs within one to five years, and warns of wealth concentration exceeding that of the Gilded Age, with personal fortunes potentially reaching into the trillions of dollars.[35]\r\n\r\nThe fifth category encompasses indirect effects and unknown factors, including rapid advances in biology that could alter human lifespans or human intelligence, unhealthy changes to human life from AI interaction, and challenges to human purpose in a world where AI exceeds human capabilities across virtually all domains.[30]\r\n\r\nDispute with the Department of Defense\r\nMain article: Anthropic–United States Department of Defense dispute\r\nIn February 2026, Amodei refused to remove a contractual ban on Claude usage for mass domestic surveillance or fully autonomous weapons, as requested by Pete Hegseth's Department of Defense (DoD). Anthropic was subsequently labeled a DoD "supply-chain risk" and the Trump administration ordered US agencies to stop using Claude. The Pentagon denied interest in such use cases but insisted on unrestricted access.[36] The DoD's actions have been widely condemned as illegal retaliation against protected speech with several organizations filing amicus briefs supporting Anthropic. On March 26, 2026, a federal judge issued a temporary injunction against the DoD, agreeing their actions appeared to be "classic First Amendment retaliation."[37][38][39]\r\n\r\nPersonal life\r\nAmodei began dating Camilla Clark in 2014 and married her in 2022.[40]\r\n\r\nReferences\r\n Levy, Steven. "If Anthropic Succeeds, a Nation of Benevolent AI Geniuses Could Be Born". Wired. ISSN 1059-1028. Retrieved March 28, 2025.\r\n Perrigo, Billy (May 30, 2024). "Inside Anthropic, the AI Company Betting That Safety Can Be a Winning Strategy". Time. Retrieved October 3, 2025.\r\n Roose, Kevin (July 11, 2023). "Inside the White-Hot Center of A.I. Doomerism". The New York Times.\r\n Oreskovic, Alexei (July 11, 2023). "Anthropic CEO A.I. risks: short, medium, and long-term". Fortune.\r\n Kessler, Sarah (December 7, 2025). "Dario Amodei on the Risk of an A.I. Bubble, Regulation and A.G.I." The New York Times. Retrieved December 9, 2025.\r\n Amodei, Dario (October 11, 2024). "Dario Amodei — Machines of Loving Grace". Retrieved November 18, 2024.\r\n Sullivan, Mark (October 17, 2024). "Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei pens a smart look at our AI future". Fast Company.\r\n Murgia, Madhumita (December 5, 2024). "Anthropic's Dario Amodei: Democracies must maintain the lead in AI". Financial Times. Retrieved August 12, 2025.\r\n Levy, Steven (March 28, 2025). "If Anthropic Succeeds, a Nation of Benevolent AI Geniuses Could Be Born". Wired. ISSN 1059-1028. Retrieved February 21, 2026.\r\n Frasso, Edoardo (November 27, 2023). "Chi sono Dario e Daniela Amodei: gli italoamericani dell'AI etica". AI News (in Italian). Retrieved February 27, 2026.\r\n Katz, Leslie (March 6, 1998). "Magnes-owned site to house Berkeleys main library". J. The Jewish News of Northern California. Retrieved June 18, 2026. Library staff spent eight months looking at possible temporary sites, according to Elena Engel, the library's project manager for the renovation.\r\n Engel, Elena (2006). "RFID Implementations in California Libraries: Costs and Benefits" (PDF). Karen Coyle. Retrieved June 18, 2026.\r\n "Lowell Alumni Newsletter Winter 2008 by Lowell Alumni Association". Issuu. August 23, 2014. Archived from the original on February 21, 2025. Retrieved July 17, 2024.\r\n "2000 US Physics olympiad team".\r\n Fuller-Wright, Liz (September 12, 2023). "TIME Magazine's TIME100 artificial intelligence list honors six Princetonians". Princeton University.\r\n Amodei, Dario (2011). Network-Scale Electrophysiology: Measuring and Understanding the Collective Behavior of Neural Circuits (Thesis).\r\n "Dario Amodei, PhD". The Hertz Foundation.\r\n Griffiths, Brent D. "Meet Dario Amodei, Anthropic's outspoken CEO". Business Insider. Retrieved October 3, 2025.\r\n "Dario Amodei". LinkedIn.\r\n Hao, Karen (February 17, 2020). "The messy, secretive reality behind OpenAI's bid to save the world". MIT Technology Review.\r\n "TIME100 AI 2023: Dario and Daniela Amodei". Time. September 7, 2023. Archived from the original on June 15, 2025. Retrieved October 3, 2025.\r\n Varanasi, Lakshmi (December 4, 2025). "Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei drags OpenAI and Google: 'We don't have to do any code reds'". Business Insider. Retrieved December 9, 2025.\r\n Goldman, Sharon (April 7, 2023). "As Anthropic seeks billions to take on OpenAI, 'industrial capture' is nigh. Or is it?". VentureBeat.\r\n Dastin, Jeffrey (November 21, 2023). "OpenAI's board approached Anthropic CEO about top job and merger". Reuters.\r\n Kutcher, Ashton (April 16, 2025). "Dario Amodei: The 100 Most Influential People of 2025". TIME. Retrieved April 18, 2025.\r\n Perrigo, Billy (April 15, 2026). "The 100 Most Influential People of 2026 | Dario Amodei and Daniela Amodei". TIME. Retrieved May 6, 2026.\r\n Rahman-Jones, Imran; McMahon, Liv (December 10, 2025). "'Architects of AI' named Time Magazine's Person of the Year". BBC News.\r\n "Anthropic raises $65B in Series H funding at $965B post-money valuation". www.anthropic.com. Retrieved May 29, 2026.\r\n "Dario Amodei". Forbes. Retrieved June 10, 2026.\r\n Amodei, Dario (January 2026). "Dario Amodei — The Adolescence of Technology".\r\n Milmo, Dan (January 27, 2026). "Wake up to the risks of AI, they are almost here, Anthropic boss warns". The Guardian. Retrieved January 29, 2026.\r\n "Issue #10". Research Office. West Virginia University. Retrieved August 17, 2026.\r\n Hammond, George; Heikkilä, Melissa (January 26, 2026). "'Humanity needs to wake up' to dangers of AI, says Anthropic chief". Financial Times.\r\n Manson, Katrina (February 25, 2026). "Anthropic's Pentagon Showdown Is About More Than AI Guardrails". Bloomberg. Retrieved February 25, 2026.\r\n Vlamis, Kelsey (September 18, 2025). "Anthropic cofounders say the likelihood of AI replacing human jobs is so high that they needed to warn the world about it". Business Insider. Retrieved October 21, 2025.\r\n "Trump orders US agencies to stop using Anthropic technology in clash over AI safety". AP News. February 27, 2026. Retrieved March 11, 2026.\r\n Tuccille, J. D. (March 11, 2026). "The federal government's crusade against Anthropic raises First Amendment concerns". Reason.com. Retrieved March 31, 2026.\r\n "The Pentagon's Retaliation Campaign Against Anthropic Is Unconstitutional". www.cato.org. March 10, 2026. Retrieved March 31, 2026.\r\n Brown, Elizabeth Nolan (March 30, 2026). "Government actions against Anthropic are 'classic First Amendment retaliation'". Reason.com. Retrieved March 31, 2026.\r\n Weinberg, Cory; McEvoy, Jemima; Lessin, Jessica; Palazzolo, Stephanie (August 13, 2026). "Anthropic's 'First Lady' Took a Winding Road to the Top". The Information.	{"size": 12031, "mimetype": "text/markdown"}	failed	{"error":{"code":503,"message":"This model is currently experiencing high demand. Spikes in demand are usually temporary. Please try again later.","status":"UNAVAILABLE"}}	2026-08-26 18:43:42.819431+05:30	2026-08-26 19:17:29.767339+05:30
