 \nthan shareholders)\nYes:   Link-- Not Applicable-- Not Applicable\nEmployees and \nworkers**\nYes   (Links: \nPrevention \nof Sexual \nHarassment at \nthe Workplace \npolicy  ,  \nVigil mechanism  \nand whistleblower \npoli\ncy ,  \nHealth and safety \npoli\ncy and  \nHuman rights \npolicy )\n325  POSH, human \nrights, working \nconditions, \nhealth & \nsafety and \nwhistleblower \ncomplaints \nhave been \nconsidered.\n1132  POSH, human \nrights, working \nconditions, \nhealth & \nsafety and \nwhistleblower \ncomplaints \nhave been \nconsidered.\nCustomers \n(End-users \nand restaurant \npartners)\nYes:   Link134239Legal cases \nfiled before \nvarious courts \nhave been \nconsidered.\n100177   Legal cases \nfiled before \nvarious courts \nhave been \nconsidered.\nValue Chain \nPartners (Delivery \npartners and \nsuppliers/vendors) \nYes:   Link416  Legal cases \nfiled before \nany court of \nlaw have been \nconsidered.\n816  Legal cases \nfiled before \nany court of \nlaw have been \nconsidered.\nOther (please \nspecify)\n-\n    *The number of complaints pending resolution at close of the year FY26 also includes the unresolved complaints from FY25.  \n    **Eternal has developed a robust mechanism for recording employee and worker complaints. We continue to educate and sensitize \nour employees and workers regarding the channels for reporting such complaints. \n\n90\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\n26. Overview of the entity’s material responsible business conduct issues\n    Please  indicate  material  responsible  business  conduct  and  sustainability  issues  pertaining  to  \nenvironmental and social matters that present a risk or an opportunity to your business, rationale for \nidentifying the same, approach to adapt or mitigate the risk along-with its financial implications, as per \nthe following format:  \n    In F\nY26, we conducted a double materiality assessment with an independent external organization to \nre-assess our sustainability priorities in line with evolving business needs and stakeholder expectations. \nThe assessment evaluated sustainability issues through the dual lens of impact and financial materiality, \nenabling us to understand both the impact our business has on people and the environment, as well as the \npotential implications of sustainability-related issues on our business performance and long-term value \ncreation. The outcome of the exercise for Eternal Limited on a standalone basis is reflected in the list of \nmaterial topics below organized under Environment, Social and Governance categories.\nS. \nNo.\nMaterial \nidentified \nissue\nIn\ndicate \nwhether \nrisk or \nopportunity \n(R /O)\nRationale for  \nidentifying the  \nrisk/ opportunity\nIn c\nase of risk, approach to adapt or \nmitigate\nFinancial implications \nof the risk or \nopportunity\n(Indicate positive or \nnegative implications)\n1.Climate action \nand circular \neconomy \nRiskClimate action and \ncircular economy \nare critical to the \nbusiness’s long-\nterm growth and \nresilience. Climate \nchange effects, \nsuch as rising \ntemperature, \nchanging \nprecipitation \npatterns and \nextreme weather \nevents, pose a \nrisk to business \noperations, \nimpacting last-mile \nfood deliveries \nand customer \ndining experience. \nEfficient use of \nenergy, water and \nother resources \npromotes resource \ncircularity, reduces \nwaste and helps \nmitigate risks \nassociated with \nresource scarcity. \n Periodic Climate Risk Assessment-\n            In FY24, we undertook a climate risk \nassessment exercise for our food \nordering and delivery business. We \nassessed the impact of physical climate \nrisks such as cyclones, storms, heavy \nprecipitation, increasing/ decreasing \ntemperature, flooding and heat/ cold \nwaves and transitional risks across our \ntop 15 cities under IPCC RCP 2.6 and \nRCP 8.5 scenarios*. The outcome of this \nexercise has been integrated into Eternal \nLimited’s Enterprise Risk Management \n(ERM) process. We have also published a \nTCFD report on Eternal’s Food Ordering \nand Delivery Business in the \n \nImpact section of Eternal’s website.\n\n Transition to EV-based food deliveries-\n  Our key climate risk mitigation action is to \nsupport delivery partners in transitioning \nto EV bikes for undertaking food \ndeliveries. To achieve this, we generate \nawareness about EVs among delivery \npartners, collaborate with EV ecosystem \nplayers to secure access for delivery \npartners to EVs on rent and advocate for \nbetter EV products, infrastructure and \nfinancing. In FY26, total EV-based food \ndeliveries increased by 50% compared to \nthe previous year, totaling 132.3 million \norders. This represented 13.5% of total \nFY26 food delivery orders.\n            For more details, please refer to \n \nPrinciple 6, Essential indicator 8\nNe\ng\native implication-\nIgnoring climate \nchange risks and \nfailure to adopt circular \neconomy practices \ncan result in increased \noperational and supply \nchain costs, regulatory \nfines, declining \ncustomer preference \nand reputational \ndamage, resulting \nin negative financial \nimpact.\n\n91\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nS. \nNo.\nMaterial \nidentified \nissue\nIn\ndicate \nwhether \nrisk or \nopportunity \n(R /O)\nRationale for  \nidentifying the  \nrisk/ opportunity\nIn c\nase of risk, approach to adapt or \nmitigate\nFinancial implications \nof the risk or \nopportunity\n(Indicate positive or \nnegative implications)\nClimate action \nand circular \neconomy \nRisk     Climate adaptation support for Delivery \npartners- We expanded our Automatic \nWeather Station (AWS) network to \n1,150 devices across 100 cities to guide \nbusiness decisions, predict weather \nconditions and communicate them to \ndelivery partners. Additionally, a network \nof merchant outlets was made available \nas rest points for delivery partners, \nenabling access to basic amenities such \nas drinking water, rest areas, washrooms \nand mobile charging facilities during \ntheir workday. The network was scaled to \n3,395 locations in FY26. We also provide \naccess to custom-made and off-the-\nshelf apparel that protects partners from \nthe unpredictability of changing climate, \nincluding raincoats and cooling vests.\n   In FY26, 3,000 cooling vest jackets \nwere provided under a pilot to delivery \npartners to assess their effectiveness \nin reducing heat stress and to provide \ndesign feedback to the manufacturer.\n\n     Carbon market instruments- We \nmaintained net GHG emissions from our \noperations (classified as Scope 1 & Scope \n2) at zero by procuring verified carbon \nremoval offsets equivalent to 100% of \nour Scope 1 emissions and International \nRenewable Energy Certificates (IRECs) \nfor Scope 2 emissions. \n\n  C\nircular  economy- Waste management \nis crucial to reducing the environmental \nimpact of our value chain operations. \nAcross our own offices, we have \nimplemented responsible waste disposal \nand water usage initiatives. For the \nfood delivery value chain we run several \nvoluntary initiatives:\n         Waste reduction- ‘Don’t send cutlery’ \nand the ‘Food Rescue’ initiatives are \ntwo key features within the Zomato \napp that help avoid the waste from \nfood delivery operations. Allowing \nusers to opt out of receiving cutlery \nwith their order has helped avoid \n~3,000 MT of cutlery waste over 4 \nyears. The Food Rescue feature \nenables cancelled but untampered \nfood orders to be made available \nto nearby customers at discounted \nprices, helping reduce food wastage. \nSince launch in FY25, Zomato’s \nFood Rescue feature has helped \nprevent over 7.5 lakh kg of food \nwaste by diverting cancelled orders \nto interested buyers (assuming each \norder weighs approximately 400g).\n\n92\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nS. \nNo.\nMaterial \nidentified \nissue\nIn\ndicate \nwhether \nrisk or \nopportunity \n(R /O)\nRationale for  \nidentifying the  \nrisk/ opportunity\nIn c\nase of risk, approach to adapt or \nmitigate\nFinancial implications \nof the risk or \nopportunity\n(Indicate positive or \nnegative implications)\nClimate action \nand circular \neconomy \nRisk         Sustainable packaging-  We host \nan annual event to bring attention \nto sustainable packaging solutions \nrelevant for food delivery. Restaurants \nthat adopt sustainable alternatives to \nplastic are also rewarded with a ‘Low \nplastic packaging’ tag on the Zomato \napp. As on March 31\nst\n 2026, 100 million \n‘Low plastic packaging’ orders had \nbeen delivered via Zomato.\n   Voluntary  recycling- We have \nvoluntarily recycled 60,000 MT of \nplastic waste on behalf of restaurants \nfrom FY23-FY26 (15,000 MT was \nrecycled in FY26) fully meeting \nthe target of 100% plastic neutral \ndeliveries as per independent \nestimates. \n            For more details, please refer to \n \nPrinciple 6, Essential indicator 10\n2.Hu\nman capital \ndevelopment\nOpportunity  \n& Risk \nExcellence in \nhuman capital \ndevelopment can \nresult in strong \nemployer branding \nand a better culture \nof innovation. \nFailure to prioritize \nhuman capital \ndevelopment, \nincluding \nattracting and \nretaining a diverse \ntalent pool and \nsupporting \nupskilling, \ncan constrain \ninnovation and \nlimit the company’s \nability to respond \nto evolving market \ndemands. \n  Talent  discovery- We follow a non-\ntraditional approach to talent discovery \nthat depends mainly on employee \nreferrals which, in our experience, \nimproves the quality of hires. We also \nleverage traditional channels such as \nnetworking sites, job portals, campus \nplacements and partnerships with \nexternal organizations to tap into diverse \npools of talent.\n\n     Championing a culture of learning \nby doing- To support fast-paced \ninnovation, Eternal’s culture emphasizes \nownership and autonomy over technical \nqualifications. The organization actively \nsupports the movement of people into \nroles where they may not have prior \nexposure but have the willingness and \ncommitment to learn. Employees are \nencouraged to demonstrate a founders’ \nmindset and take ownership of problems \nwell outside their department or comfort \nzone. We reward employees for both \npositive outcomes and failures. We \nalso invest in learning and development \nprograms - both offline and online - to \nenable employees to upskill for evolving \nbusiness requirements.\nPositive and negative \nimplication- \nStrong performance \nin human capital \ndevelopment results \nin better talent and \nlower hiring costs. \nGaps in human capital \ndevelopment can lead \nto higher recruitment \nand training costs, \nwhile limited workforce \ndiversity may hinder \nability to respond \nto evolving market \ndemands. \n\n93\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nS. \nNo.\nMaterial \nidentified \nissue\nIn\ndicate \nwhether \nrisk or \nopportunity \n(R /O)\nRationale for  \nidentifying the  \nrisk/ opportunity\nIn c\nase of risk, approach to adapt or \nmitigate\nFinancial implications \nof the risk or \nopportunity\n(Indicate positive or \nnegative implications)\nHuman capital \ndevelopment\nOpportunity  \n& Risk \n        Fostering Inclusion- Diversity is \nsupported by Eternal’s culture. The \norganization operates with a flat, non-\nhierarchical structure that generates \na lot of leadership opportunities for \nyounger people and those from diverse \ngroups. Our Employee Resource \nGroups (ERGs) and online communities \norganized around identity, life stage \nand interest, help foster belonging and \nengagement among diverse groups \nof employees. Our Equal opportunity, \ndiversity and inclusion policy and \nassociated SoPs, ensure fair, non-\ndiscriminatory treatment for all \nemployees.\n            By endorsing global standards such \nas the UN Women’s Empowerment \nPrinciples (UN WEPs) and the UN \nStandards of Conduct for Business \nTackling Discrimination against \nLGBTQIA+ people, we affirm our \ndedication to fostering an inclusive \nworkplace. \n        Open channels for employee \ncommunication- Eternal is committed \nto maintaining transparent and \naccessible channels for employees to \ncommunicate with each other and with \nleadership. We encourage employee \ncommunication and support grievance \nredressal via a variety of offline and \nonline channels such as townhalls, \nslack, email and surveys. All leaders \nmaintain an open-door policy. Formal \nprocesses are in place to address \nconcerns related to sexual harassment, \nwhistleblower complaints, workplace \nconduct and human rights issues in a \nfair, confidential and timely manner.\n        Continuous feedback and 360-degree \nperformance appraisal- Eternal follows \na 360-degree performance appraisal \nmethod that provides a comprehensive \nview of an employee’s strengths, \nweaknesses and blind spots, enabling \ntargeted human capital development \ninterventions. While formal \nperformance appraisals are conducted \nannually, managers and employees are \nencouraged to share feedback more \nfrequently to facilitate faster learning, \ncourse correction and recognition. \n\n94\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nS. \nNo.\nMaterial \nidentified \nissue\nIn\ndicate \nwhether \nrisk or \nopportunity \n(R /O)\nRationale for  \nidentifying the  \nrisk/ opportunity\nIn c\nase of risk, approach to adapt or \nmitigate\nFinancial implications \nof the risk or \nopportunity\n(Indicate positive or \nnegative implications)\n3.Health, safety, \nand dignity  \nfor all\nRiskEnsuring a safe and \nrespectful working \nenvironment \nis core to how \nwe operate. \nInadequate health \n& safety measures \ncan lead to injuries \nor casualties \naffecting well-\nbeing. This may \nalso lead to higher \nattrition and loss \nof stakeholder \ntrust. Failure to \nuphold human \nrights, including \nhealth & safety, \ncan further expose \nthe company to \nregulatory risks, \nwith implications \nfor business \ncontinuity.\n     Employees and workers- Our \noccupational health & safety framework \nis supported through a comprehensive \npolicy and robust workplace safety \nsystems. These include mandatory \ntraining for employees and workers \non health & safety, fire detection \n& fire fighting mechanisms, safety \nsignage and the Safety Reporting \nSystem (SRS) for proactive incident \nreporting and resolution. We have \nsecured ISO 45001:2018 certification \nfor the Occupational Health and Safety \nManagement systems in our Gurugram \nand Bengaluru leased offices. Please \nrefer to Principle 3, Essential indicator 10 \nfor more details.\n\n Delivery partners\n         Road safety awareness and training- \nAll delivery partners are required to \ncomplete a mandatory road safety \nawareness module at the time of \nonboarding. This is supplemented by \nperiodic health & safety programs \nconducted through both online and \noffline formats. Delivery partners \nalso receive educational content and \nvideos on safe riding practices under \nvaried weather conditions such as rain, \nheat and fog. In addition, we regularly \norganize safety camps in collaboration \nwith local traffic authorities and \nhave partnered with the Ministry of \nRoad Transport and Highways for the \nnational campaign ‘Sadak Suraksha \nAbhiyan’ promoting responsible road \nbehavior among delivery partners and \nencouraging customers not to rush \nthem.\n         \nSOS service- An SOS service, \naccessible via the delivery partner \napp, is available to provide immediate \nsupport to all delivery partners in \ncase of emergencies, including \naccidents, vehicle breakdowns, etc. \nThe SOS service is set up to provide \nfree ambulance to partners in 900+ \ncities, depending on the emergency. \nAmbulances are positioned to reach \npartners within 20 minutes.\n         \nInsurance coverage- Eternal \nvoluntarily provides accidental and \nmedical insurance coverage, inclusive \nof disability and death benefits for all \nactive delivery partners. The premium \nfor this insurance is borne entirely by \nEternal. \nNegative implication-\nInadequate health \n& safety measures \nacross operations can \nincrease the risk of \nwork-related injuries or \ncasualties, adversely \naffecting well-being. \nFailure to uphold \nhuman rights, including \nthe right to safe \nworking conditions, \nmay lead to business \ndisruptions, regulatory \npenalties, legal \nliabilities and cause \nreputational damage, \nresulting in negative \nfinancial impact. \n\n95\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nS. \nNo.\nMaterial \nidentified \nissue\nIn\ndicate \nwhether \nrisk or \nopportunity \n(R /O)\nRationale for  \nidentifying the  \nrisk/ opportunity\nIn c\nase of risk, approach to adapt or \nmitigate\nFinancial implications \nof the risk or \nopportunity\n(Indicate positive or \nnegative implications)\nHealth, safety, \nand dignity  \nfor all\nRisk         No time-based pressure- Delivery \npartners are only shown the distance \nto the customer’s location and are \nnot shown the delivery time promised \nto customers, helping reduce time-\nbased pressure while on the road.\n         Rest points- A network of merchant \noutlets is available as rest points for \ndelivery partners, enabling access \nto basic amenities such as drinking \nwater, rest areas, washrooms and \nmobile charging facilities. The \nnetwork was scaled to 3,395 locations \nin FY26.\n         Black zone reporting- Delivery \npartners can report unsafe or difficult-\nto-navigate areas through the delivery \npartner app. More than 270 black \nzones were identified as on 31\nst\n March \n2026, with such locations marked as \nunserviceable, where required, to \nenhance safety.\n\n Upholding human rights \n         Zero tolerance- Eternal has a strict \nzero-tolerance policy for any form \nof discrimination or harassment \n(including sexual harassment). The \npolicy applies to all internal and \nexternal stakeholders, including \ndelivery partners, restaurant \npartners, customers and Eternal \nemployees and workers.  \n         Third party human rights assessment \nand regular surveys- In FY26, Human \nRights Assessment was carried out by \nan independent third party consultant \nwhich included a comprehensive \nevaluation of the organization’s \npolicies, practices and procedures \nand no material gaps were identified \nduring the assessment. We also \nconducted a large-scale pan-India \nDelivery Partner Wellbeing survey that \ncovered topics of health, safety and \ndignity. \n   Accessibility for Persons with \nDisabilities (PwD)- We conduct \nperiodic assessments for our directly \nleased offices and all new facilities \nunder our direct lease have been \nevaluated for accessibility standards \noutlined in the RPwD Act, 2016.  \n   Pl\nease refer to our Human rights policy \nand Health and safety policy for more \ndetails. \n\n96\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nS. \nNo.\nMaterial \nidentified \nissue\nIn\ndicate \nwhether \nrisk or \nopportunity \n(R /O)\nRationale for  \nidentifying the  \nrisk/ opportunity\nIn c\nase of risk, approach to adapt or \nmitigate\nFinancial implications \nof the risk or \nopportunity\n(Indicate positive or \nnegative implications)\n4.Innovation\nOpportunityContinuous \ninnovation \nacross platform \ntechnology, product \nand services \nenhances customer \nexperience, \nimproves \noperational \nefficiency, enables \ndiscovery of \nnew business \nopportunities and \nsupports Eternal’s \ncompetitive \npositioning and \nlong-term growth.\nEternal is constantly testing and launching \ninnovations in technology, product and \nservice offerings across its businesses. \nFor the standalone entity, in FY26, some \nnotable, recent innovations include:\n\n  N\nugget- Eternal introduced Nugget in \nFY25 as an AI-native customer support \nplatform that autonomously resolves \ncustomer grievances delivering faster \nresolutions, personalized support, higher \ncustomer satisfaction and significant \ncost savings. Since then, Nugget has \nevolved beyond support into building \nconversational AI experiences for \nenterprises across different sectors, \nthrough voice and non-voice agents that \nwork together to replicate full contact \ncentre operations. Nugget today powers \nover 15 million automated interactions \nevery month. \n            At Eternal, we have deployed AI across \nour applications to enhance platform \nexperience. \n  Healthy  mode- To capture the growing \ndemand for healthier meals, Eternal \nlaunched “Healthy mode” on the \nZomato app in top 7 cities in FY26. With \nthis mode, end users  see a curation \nof  restaurants along with complete \nnutritional information, i.e. macros, \ningredient-level description and \ncustomization filters. Since its launch, \nHealthy Mode has helped customers \nmake more informed and healthier food \nchoices, generating ~8.5 million orders \n \nin FY26. \nPo\nsitive implication-\nInnovations in \ntechnology, product \nand service enhance \nplatform experience \nfor our customers \nand delivery \npartners- providing \nus a competitive edge \nwhile contributing to \nlong-term financial \nperformance.\n\n97\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nS. \nNo.\nMaterial \nidentified \nissue\nIn\ndicate \nwhether \nrisk or \nopportunity \n(R /O)\nRationale for  \nidentifying the  \nrisk/ opportunity\nIn c\nase of risk, approach to adapt or \nmitigate\nFinancial implications \nof the risk or \nopportunity\n(Indicate positive or \nnegative implications)\nInnovation\nOpportunity        Chef’s table experience- A thoughtfully \ncurated multi-course dining-at-\nhome experience from some of the \nfinest restaurants, delivered directly \nto customers’ homes. Each menu \nis designed to bring the restaurant \nexperience home, with premium course-\nwise packaging for a ‘Meal for 2’. The \nprogram is live in Delhi NCR, Mumbai and \nBengaluru across 250+ restaurant brands \nand 650+ outlets.\n\n        Zo\nmato for Enterprise (ZFE) - ZFE is \na platform designed for companies to \nsolve food expense management. A lot \nof Zomato orders placed by corporate \nemployees are business-related and need \nto be reimbursed by their employer via \nprocesses that are often, cumbersome \nand time-consuming. With ZFE, \nemployees can simply bill their business \norders directly to their employer, without \nhaving to pay, adding convenience \nfor employees and transparency for \ncompanies. Over 350 companies have \nadopted ZFE.\n\n        Food on Train- Long-distance Indian rail \ncarries enormous passenger volumes, \nyet onboard catering has limited choices \nand inconsistent quality. Zomato’s Food \non Train service fixes this through its \nIRCTC partnership: customers order \non the app, enter their PNR, and pick a \nstation along the route, and Zomato’s \nstation walkers hand the order over right \nat the coach gate. Delivering this service \nmeant building real-time train tracking \nand a dedicated layer of walker logistics \nsynced to each station halt. Zomato \ndelivers at 250+ stations, pan-India.\n\n98\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nS. \nNo.\nMaterial \nidentified \nissue\nIn\ndicate \nwhether \nrisk or \nopportunity \n(R /O)\nRationale for  \nidentifying the  \nrisk/ opportunity\nIn c\nase of risk, approach to adapt or \nmitigate\nFinancial implications \nof the risk or \nopportunity\n(Indicate positive or \nnegative implications)\n5.Stakeholder \nvalue creation \nOpportunityAs a platform-\nled business, \ncreating shared \nvalue across \nour ecosystem \nof restaurant \npartners, delivery \npartners, \nsuppliers, \ncustomers and  \nbusiness partners, \nis integral to our \nlong-term growth \nand resilience. By \nenabling income \ngeneration  \nopportunities, \nexpanding market \naccess, fostering \nfinancial inclusion \nand promoting \nresponsible \nsourcing practices, \nwe strengthen the \nresilience of our \necosystem while \nenhancing service \nquality, business \ncontinuity and \nstakeholder trust.\nBelow are examples of how we create value \nfor stakeholders:\n        Restaurant partners\n   In FY26, Eternal’s food ordering and \ndelivery business generated earnings \nthrough delivery for 5 lakh+ restaurant \npartners, 2.45 lakh+ of which are \ndelivery-only restaurants \n    Restaurant Services Hub- In 2024, \nwe launched a voluntary initiative \nthat supports restaurants in finding \nvendors for special equipment \n \n(such as induction stoves), staffing, \nse\ncuring FSSAI licenses and \nconducting hygiene audits. In \n \nFY26, 37,000 restaurants availed \nse\nrvices via the hub.\n\n  Delivery  partners\n   In F\nY26, Eternal’s food ordering and \ndelivery business generated earnings \nthrough delivery for over 18.5 lakh unique \nindividuals. Beyond generating earnings, \nwe create value via special initiatives\n   Fin\nancial inclusion and social \nsecurity for delivery partners-  \nDelivery partners associated with \nEternal have access to free-of-cost \nmedical and accidental private \ninsurance, including OPD treatment, \nhospitalization cover and loss of pay \nsupport. We also organize drives \nto enrol partners into relevant \ngovernment schemes and insurance \nprograms, support them in tax filing \nand in accessing financial services \nand products such as fixed and \nrecurring deposits and pension \nschemes via the delivery partner app. \n   Fo\nr more details, please refer Principle 4, \nLeadership indicator 2\n         Wo\nmen delivery partner initiatives- \nEternal has targeted initiatives to \nsupport women’s participation in \nlast-mile delivery beyond the flexibility \nprovided by the platform to all \npartners. These include the option to \nundertake short-distance deliveries \nonly, period rest days, additional \nsafety measures, maternity benefits \nand Women’s Riding Centers focused \non 2-wheeler and digital literacy \ntraining in select cities. As a result, \nwomen’s participation in Zomato’s \ndelivery partner fleet grew to over \n3,500 in March 2026. \nFor more details, please refer Principle 4, \nLeadership indicator 3 \nPositive implication- \nInvestments in \nstrengthening \nlivelihoods, \nexpanding economic \nopportunities, enabling \nfinancial inclusion \nand integration of \nresponsible sourcing \npractices contributes \nto higher stakeholder \nretention, improved \nservice quality and \ngreater operational \nstability, enhancing the \ncompany’s financial \nperformance.  \n\n99\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nS. \nNo.\nMaterial \nidentified \nissue\nIn\ndicate \nwhether \nrisk or \nopportunity \n(R /O)\nRationale for  \nidentifying the  \nrisk/ opportunity\nIn c\nase of risk, approach to adapt or \nmitigate\nFinancial implications \nof the risk or \nopportunity\n(Indicate positive or \nnegative implications)\nStakeholder \nvalue creation \nOpportunity         Livelihood program for Persons with \nDisabilities (PwD)- Launched in FY23, \nour PwD delivery partner initiative \nhas generated earnings for over 6,000 \nPwDs with a range of disabilities, \nincluding locomotor disabilities and \nhearing and speech impairment. \nIn FY26 alone, 3,500+ PwD delivery \npartners, collectively delivered over \n10 million orders and covered over \n40 million kms. The program benefits \ninclude waiver of onboarding fee \nand assignment of short-distance \ndeliveries only.  Eternal received a \nNational Award for empowering PwDs \nfrom the President of India in 2024 for \nthis initiative. \n   For more details, please refer Principle 4, \nLeadership indicator 3\n Suppliers\n         Sustainable supply chain program- \nAll our supplier relationships are \ngoverned by our Business Partner \nCode of Conduct and Responsible \nSourcing Policy.\n    In FY25, we also launched a \n‘Sustainable Supply Chain Program’ \nto strengthen responsible sourcing \nacross our value chain. Through \nthis program, we assess suppliers \non key ESG parameters, upskill \nthem on reporting, performance \nand implement a standardized ESG \nscreening framework. The program \naims to embed sustainability \nconsiderations into procurement \ndecisions while recognizing suppliers \nthat align with mutual sustainability \nvalues and sourcing expectations.\n   Fo\nr more details, please refer Principle 2, \nEssential indicator 2\n\n    C\nommunities\n         Feeding India- Eternal’s apps enable \ncustomers to contribute to Feeding \nIndia, a not-for-profit initiative, \ndirectly at the checkout page. These \ncontributions support Feeding India’s \nmission focused on eradicating hunger \nand malnutrition among children from \nunder-served communities across \nthe country. Feeding India supports \nmeals for 1.4 lakh+ children daily and \nhas served over 290 million meals since \ninception till FY26.\nFor more details, please refer Principle 4, \nLeadership indicator 3 \n\n100\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nS. \nNo.\nMaterial \nidentified \nissue\nIn\ndicate \nwhether \nrisk or \nopportunity \n(R /O)\nRationale for  \nidentifying the  \nrisk/ opportunity\nIn c\nase of risk, approach to adapt or \nmitigate\nFinancial implications \nof the risk or \nopportunity\n(Indicate positive or \nnegative implications)\n6.Data privacy, \ncybersecurity \nand \nresponsible AI\nRisk Inadequate \ncybersecurity, \ndata privacy and \nAI governance \nmeasures may \nexpose the \norganization to \nvarious cyber \nthreats/ incidents, \nincluding \nransomware \nattacks, phishing \nscams, Denial-\nof-Service (DoS) \nattacks, and \nothers, which \nmay result in data \nbreaches, service \ndisruptions, \nand the loss of \nconfidential or \nsensitive data of \nkey stakeholders, \nincluding \nPersonally \nIdentifiable \nInformation (PII) \nwhich may lead to \nloss of stakeholder \ntrust.\n  Information security policy and \nframework- Eternal has formulated \nan information security policy and \nframework to protect Eternal’s data \nand information from unauthorized \naccess and external threats. A dedicated \ninformation security team is responsible \nfor monitoring the effectiveness of our \ncybersecurity policies and systems.\n\n        ISO Certification- Eternal is committed \nto adhering to global best practices \nfor data protection and continues to \nhold ISO  27001:2022 certification for \nthe information security management \nsystems. These practices include annual \nmandatory cybersecurity awareness for \nall employees.\n\n  P\neriodic  assessment- Eternal \nprioritizes continuous improvement in \ncybersecurity via thorough and periodic \nreview mechanisms. These include \nindependent assessments such as \naudits, Vulnerability Assessment and \nPenetration Testing (VAPT) evaluations, \nthird party reviews and bug bounty \nprograms. These initiatives help us  stay \nvigilant and proactive in safeguarding our \nsystems and data against evolving cyber \nthreats.\n\n     Investment in technologies- We invest \nin advanced cybersecurity and data \nprotection technologies, reinforcing \nthe control framework to mitigate \nexisting and emerging risks effectively. \nWe conduct regular company-wide \nawareness sessions to educate and \nempower all employees with the latest \nbest practices, fostering a culture of \nvigilance and resilience across our \norganization.\n\n     Responsible AI governance - We are \ncommitted to the responsible and ethical \nuse of artificial intelligence across our \nbusiness. User privacy remains a priority, \nwith all data used for AI development \nand deployment maintained in secure, \naccess-controlled environments \nand managed in line with ethical data \npractices. Real-time monitoring \nprotocols track model performance, \nvalidate accuracy, and proactively \ndetect and address algorithmic bias or \ndata drift. AI-generated responses and \noutputs are clearly labelled so users know \nwhen they are interacting with artificial \nintelligence. We also conduct employee \ntraining and awareness initiatives to help \nthem understand the ethical, legal, and \nsafety considerations associated with AI.\nNegative implication-\nIneffective \ncybersecurity and AI \ngovernance measures \nmay lead to data \nbreaches, service \ndisruptions, and the \nloss of confidential \nor sensitive data \nincluding PII of key \nstakeholders. This \nmay result in loss of \nstakeholder trust, \nregulatory fines, legal \nliabilities, diminished \nmarket reputation \nand negative financial \nimplications.\n\n101\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nS. \nNo.\nMaterial \nidentified \nissue\nIn\ndicate \nwhether \nrisk or \nopportunity \n(R /O)\nRationale for  \nidentifying the  \nrisk/ opportunity\nIn c\nase of risk, approach to adapt or \nmitigate\nFinancial implications \nof the risk or \nopportunity\n(Indicate positive or \nnegative implications)\n7.Brand and \nreputation\nOpportunity \n& Risk\nEternal’s corporate \nand consumer \nbrands present \nan enormous \nopportunity for \ncreating positive \nfinancial value, if \nthey are managed \nwell with adequate \nsafeguards.\nReputational \nrisks arising from \nadverse publicity, \nmisinformation, \nor misleading \ncommunication \nacross platforms \nmay negatively \nimpact stakeholder \ntrust and brand \nperception. \nInconsistent \nservice quality and \nweak grievance \nresolution may \nresult in revenue \nloss and declining \nbrand loyalty for \nEternal. \n  Communicating  responsibly- All \nour marketing and advertising \ncommunications are bound by our Code \nof Conduct and internal guidelines \nto ensure communication with \nstakeholders is ethical, transparent, \naccurate, inclusive and mindful of \ndiverse audiences. We carefully review \neach piece of communication issued by \nour company to internal and external \nstakeholders to ensure it is  truthful, \naccurate, appropriate and compliant with \napplicable brand guidelines, company \npolicies, laws and regulations.  \n\n        Multi-channel feedback collection on \nservices supporting timely response- \nWe have strong, multi-channel \nfeedback collection mechanisms in \nplace to maintain high levels of service \nquality to diverse groups of platform \nusers. Apart from teams of restaurant \naccount managers and fleet operations \nmanagers, who manage relationships \nin every city we operate in, we also have \ndedicated central customer, restaurant \nand delivery partner support teams \nthat track offline and online channels \nto ensure timely query and grievance \nresolution. In FY26, we also deployed \nAI towards faster resolution of queries \nand complaints from end users, delivery \npartners and restaurant partners. We \nregularly collect ratings from these \ngroups via the apps to continuously \nenhance their platform experience. \nCollated feedback is converted into \nactions that can enhance brand loyalty. \n   For more details please refer to Principle \n9, Leadership indicator 4.\n   Pl\nease refer to the ‘Advertising’ section \nunder our Code of Conduct for Employees \nfor more details. \nPositive and negative \nimplication-\nBeing perceived as \na fair, responsible, \ntransparent and \ndependable brand \nhelps us attract and \nretain high quality, \npremium customers \nand reliable business \npartners. Strong brand \nand reputation helps \nto lower business \ncosts and improves \nthe quality and pace \nof innovation, helping \nEternal maintain its \ncompetitive edge in \nthe market while also \nmaking a positive \ncontribution to society \nand the planet.  Clear \nand transparent \ncommunication \nreinforces trust and \nprovides support for \na long-term stable \nfinancial performance. \nNegative customer \nfeedback that remains \nunaddressed and poor \nquality of service \ncan weaken brand \ntrust and increase \nmarketing costs we will \nneed to incur to attract \nnew customers. \n8.Corporate \ngovernance, \nethics and risk \noversight\nRisk & \nOpportunity\nRobust governance \npractices, \npromoting \ntransparency, \naccountability, \nethical conduct \nand compliance \nwith laws, leads to \nminimizing risks, \npreventing financial \nmismanagement, \nenhancing investor \nconfidence, and \nfostering long-term \ngrowth.\n        Po\nlicy framework- The Company has \nestablished a comprehensive policy \nframework designed to cultivate, \npromote and sustain the highest \nstandards of ethical conduct across all \nlevels of the organization. The Company \nmandates a rigorous compliance \nprogram anchored by regular, mandatory \ntraining sessions.  \n\n        Gove\nrnance Oversight- The company \nhas instituted an Audit Committee \nand Risk Management Committee to \nprovide oversight and ensure effective \ngovernance practices.\nPositive and negative \nimplication-\nEffective governance \nand responsible \nbusiness practices \nensure enhanced and \nstructured decision-\nmaking, improved \nrisk management, \na competitive edge, \nreduced risk of \nfinancial irregularities \nor leaks and minimize \nrisk of regulatory non-\ncompliance, etc.\n\n102\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nS. \nNo.\nMaterial \nidentified \nissue\nIn\ndicate \nwhether \nrisk or \nopportunity \n(R /O)\nRationale for  \nidentifying the  \nrisk/ opportunity\nIn c\nase of risk, approach to adapt or \nmitigate\nFinancial implications \nof the risk or \nopportunity\n(Indicate positive or \nnegative implications)\nCorporate \ngovernance, \nethics and risk \noversight\nRisk & \nOpportunity\n\n        Compliance- We have a compliance tool \nwhich helps in the timely identification \nof applicable laws and regulations, \nstreamlines processes, aids in \nmonitoring and managing compliance \nactivities and provides a structured \nreporting process to uphold regulatory \nstandards, thereby facilitating better \ndecision-making.\n\n        Whis\ntleblower Mechanism- We have \nimplemented a whistleblower mechanism \nto encourage the reporting of concerns \nand potential wrongdoing, ensuring \nconfidentiality and protection against \nretaliation.\n\n        Risk Management- Implemented an \nEnterprise Risk Management (ERM) \nframework to identify strategic risks \nthat may impact the company, enabling \nproactive monitoring and mitigation \nefforts.\n9.Macroeconomic \nand geopolitical \nrisk \nRiskFluctuations in \nglobal and domestic \nmacroeconomic \nconditions, \nincluding \ngeopolitical \nvolatility, may \nimpact fuel \nprices, interest \nrates, inflation \nand economic \nstability. This can \nalter purchasing \nbehavior, dampen \ndemand, increase \ncosts and heighten \nmarket uncertainty, \nthereby affecting \nthe company’s \nrevenue and \nsustained growth.\n\n  Business diversification- Eternal has \nstrategically diversified its portfolio \nacross multiple verticals, catering to a \nbroad spectrum of customer needs, price \npoints and spending capacities. This \nreduces reliance on any single customer \nsegment, helping cushion the impact of \nmacroeconomic fluctuations.\n\n  Responsive strategy and resource \nallocation- Eternal actively monitors \nmacroeconomic trends-both short \nand long-term and adjusts its business \nstrategy accordingly. This includes \nlaunching or modifying product offerings \nbased on economic indicators and \nre-allocating resources to better align \nwith changing consumer behavior and \npreferences during volatile periods.\nNegative Implication- \nIneffective \nmanagement of \nmacroeconomic and \ngeopolitical volatility \nmay lead to rising input \ncosts, compressing \nmargins. Heightened \nuncertainty could \nalso delay strategic \ninvestments and \nexpansion plans, \nultimately impacting \nprofitability, cash flows \nand long-term growth \nprospects.\n    *Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) RCPs (Representative Concentration Pathways) are climate scenarios used \nto predict future climate conditions under different greenhouse gas emission levels. We selected a low-emissions scenario (RCP \n2.6) and a high-emissions scenario (RCP 8.5) to assess the potential impact of climate change on our operations. Reference:  \nTCFD Report FY25 (Scenario analysis, Page 10).\n\n103\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nSECTION B – MANAGEMENT AND PROCESS DISCLOSURES\nDisclosure Questions\nP1\nP2\nP3\nP4\nP5\nP6\nP7\nP8\nP9\nPolicy and Management Processes1.\n \na)\n     \nWhether your entity’s p\nolicy/ policies cover \neach principle and its core elements of the NGRBCs. (Yes/ No) \nY\nY\nY\nY\nY\nY\nY\nY\nY\n \nb)\n     \nHas the policy been a\npproved by the \nBoard? (Yes/ No)\nY\nY\nY\nY\nY\nY\nY\nY\nY\n \nc)\n     \nWeb Link of the po\nlicies, if available\nRefer to the below policies:Vigil mechanism and whistleblower policyCode of conduct for employeesAnti-bribery and anti-corruption policyPolicy to promote diversity on the board of directorsCode of conduct for board of directors and senior management personnelPolicy on materiality of Related Party Transactions and dealing with Related Party Transactions\nRefer to the below policies:Business partner code of conductResponsible sourcing policy\nRefer to the below policies:Human rights policyPrevention of Sexual Harassment at the Workplace PolicyHealth and safety policyEqual opportunity, diversity and inclusion policy\nRefer to the below policy:Stakeholder management policy \nRefer to the below policies:Nomination and remuneration policyHuman rights policy\nRefer to the below policy:Environmental policy\nRefer to the below policies:Code of conduct for employees Business partner code of conduct\nRefer to the below policies:Corporate social responsibility policyStakeholder management policy\nRefer to the below policies:Information security policy Code of conduct for employeesBusiness partner code of conductResponsible sourcing policy\n\n104\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nDisclosure Questions\nP1\nP2\nP3\nP4\nP5\nP6\nP7\nP8\nP9\n2. \n      \nWhether the entity has translated the policy into procedures.\n \n(Yes/ No)  \nY\nY\nY\nY\nY\nY\nY\nY\nY\n3. \n      \nDo the enlisted policies extend to your value chain partners? (Yes/ No)ȣ \nY\nY\nY\nY\nY\nY\nY\nY\nY\n4. \n \n \nNa\nme of the national \nand international codes/ certifications/ labels/ standards (e.g., Forest Stewardship Council, Fairtrade, Rainforest Alliance, Trustea) standards (e.g., SA 8000, OHSAS, ISO, BIS) adopted by your entity and mapped to each principle. \nEmployee CoC is aligned with the UN Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights Anti-bribery and anti-corruption policy is aligned with the Ten Principles of the UN Global Compact, Foreign Corrupt Practices Act, Prevention of Corruption Act and its amendments\nBusiness partner/ supplier CoC is aligned with the International Bill of Human Rights, ILO Declaration on Fundamental Principles and Rights at Work, OECD Guidelines for Multinational Enterprises, UN Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights (UNGPs) and the Ten Principles of the UN Global CompactResponsible sourcing policy is aligned with the National Guidelines on Responsible Business Conduct\nHealth and Safety policy is aligned with the requirements of ISO 45001 standardHuman rights, and Equal opportunity, diversity and inclusion policies are aligned with the UN Guiding Principles on Business, and Human Rights and the Ten Principles of the UN Global Compact Human Rights policy is in accordance with the Universal Declaration of Human Rights, ILO Declaration on Fundamental Principles and Rights at Work\nOur stakeholders have been identified as per the AA1000 Stakeholder Engagement Standard\nHuman rights policy is aligned with the UN Guiding Principles (UNGC) on Business, Universal Declaration of Human Rights, ILO Declaration on Fundamental Principles and Rights at Work and Human Rights, Principles of the UN Global Compact\nEnvironment policy is aligned with the requirements of the ISO 14001 Standard\nEmployee CoC & Business partner CoC is aligned with the UN Guiding Principles on Business and Human RightsEmployee CoC aligns with the Foreign Corrupt Practices Act and the Bribery ActBusiness partner CoC is aligned with the International Bill of Human Rights, the OECD Guidelines for Multinational Enterprises, and ILO Declaration on Fundamental Principles and Rights at Work\nOur stakeholders have been identified as per the AA1000 Stakeholder Engagement Standard\nInformation security policy aligns with the requirements of ISO 27001:2022 standardBusiness partner CoC and Responsible sourcing policy is aligned with the International Bill of Human Rights, ILO Declaration on Fundamental Principles and Rights at Work, OECD Guidelines for Multinational Enterprises, UN Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights (UNGPs), and the Ten Principles of the UN Global CompactResponsible sourcing policy is aligned with the National Guidelines on Responsible Business Conduct\n\n105\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nDisclosure Questions\nP1\nP2\nP3\nP4\nP5\nP6\nP7\nP8\nP9\n5. \n \n \nSp\necific commitments, \ngoals and targets set by the entity with defined timelines, if any. \nEternal’s sustainability targets and commitments are outlined on pages 33 to 43 of the Annual Report FY26.\n6.\n \n \nPe\nrformance of the \nentity against the specific commitments, goals and targets along-with reasons in case the same are not met. \nPerformance against Eternal’s sustainability targets and commitments has been discussed on pages 33 to 43 of the Annual Report FY26.\nGovernance Leadership and Oversight7. \n \n \nSt\natement by director responsible for the business responsibility report, highlighting ESG related challenges, targets and achievements (listed entity has Ŧexibility regarding \nthe placement of this disclosure) \n                    \nAt Eternal, we approach sustainability with the same ambition, patience and discipline we apply to building the business. We are cognizant of our responsibility in setting a high bar in this domain for new-age, home-grown, technology companies, like ourselves. This BRSR report showcases our progress in FY26 and reflects our commitment to grow in ways that are responsible, inclusive and sustainable. \n                    \nIn FY26, we conducted a double materiality assessment with an independent external organization to refresh our material ESG topics. The assessment considered the impact of our business on people and the environment, and the implications of sustainability-related issues on long-term value creation. Our sustainability framework has, accordingly, been refreshed and re-organized under 4 key pillars: Climate Action and Circular Economy, Inclusive Growth, Responsible Business Leadership and Corporate Governance. I’ll be sharing ESG highlights from the food delivery business as per the refreshed framework.\n                    \nOur work on \nClimate Action and Circular Economy\n focuses on minimizing our environmental impacts while preparing the business for changes in climate and resource \navailability. A key climate risk mitigation action is enabling EV-based deliveries. In FY26, total EV-based food deliveries stood at 132.3 million orders, 13.5% of total FY26 food delivery orders and a 50% increase from the previous year. This achievement rode on the back of expanded EV ecosystem partnerships and the launch of our own EV rental fleet to match the mobility needs of delivery workers, often migrants seeking to participate in the platform on a part-time and short-term basis. In addition to our own efforts, the speed of this transition will require coordinated public and private stakeholder action to ensure the right EV products, adequate charging/swapping infrastructure and affordable financing are made available to anyone who seeks to participate and earn from deliveries. \n                    \nWe are working to reduce both food and packaging waste generated by the food delivery business. Since launch in FY25, Zomato’s Food Rescue feature has helped prevent over 7.5 lakh kg of food waste by diverting cancelled orders to interested buyers. To reduce the environmental impact of restaurant packaging, we launched Zomato’s Plastic-Free Future Program in 2024 and have recognized more than 10,000 restaurants with a ‘low plastic packaging’ tag for switching out of plastic to pack their main menu items . Cumulative orders from restaurants using sustainable packaging crossed 100 million in FY26.\n                    \nOur platform facilitates \nInclusive Growth\n by lowering barriers to entry, enabling access to customers and creating livelihood opportunities through food orders and \ndelivery. In FY26, the platform generated earnings through food delivery for 5 lakh+ restaurant partners and over 18.5 lakh unique individuals. Beyond income generation, we supported delivery partners through insurance, government scheme access, tax filing support and savings-related products. In FY26, over 5,500 food delivery partners were enrolled in at least one government scheme, unlocking access to benefits valued at over INR 120 crore.\n                    \nResponsible Business Leadership \nmeans maintaining our business lead by fostering innovation while protecting stakeholder trust, safety and dignity. In FY26, we \nlaunched Healthy Mode, to help customers make informed and healthier food choices, generating around 8.5 million orders. Our human rights assessment, conducted by an independent organization, found no risks, reinforcing our strength in protecting people.  As we grew, we also enabled micro-donations to Feeding India’s network to deliver nutritionally balanced meals to 1.4 lakh children every day. The independent initiative reached a milestone of serving 290 million meals from inception till FY26.\n                    \nCorporate Governance\n, including topics of risk oversight, ethical conduct, cyber security and data privacy, remains central to decision-making at Eternal . In FY26 we \nintroduced several new measures to ensure responsible use of AI, conscious of the opportunities and risks associated with AI. Our governance and cybersecurity practices enabled us to ensure zero data breaches in FY26.\n                    \nOur efforts to date have earned us high scores in terms of ESG ratings across both Indian and global ESG rating agencies, positioning us as a leader in terms of sustainability. We will continue to report our progress transparently, remain disciplined in our approach, and revisit our assumptions as the business and the broader ecosystem evolve. More details on the ESG performance of Eternal’s non-food delivery businesses may be found on Pages 33 to 43 of our Annual Report. \n                    \n- Sanjeev Bikhchandani, Non-Executive Director, Eternal Limited\n\n106\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nDisclosure Questions\nP1\nP2\nP3\nP4\nP5\nP6\nP7\nP8\nP9\n8. \n \n \nDe\ntails of the highest \nauthority responsible for implementation and oversight of the Business Responsibility policy (ies).\nBoard of Directors\n9.\n \n \nDo\nes the entity have a \nspecified Committee of the Board/ Director responsible for decision making on sustainability related issues? (Yes / No). If yes, provide details.\nYes. The CSR committee, which is a sub-committee of Eternal’s Board of Directors, has oversight of ESG matters including sustainability and climate. The CSR committee is represented by the below members:- Sanjeev Bikhchandani, Chairman and Independent Director- Namita Gupta, Non-Executive and Independent Director- Aparna Popat Ved, Non-Executive and Independent DirectorMr. Deepinder Goyal stepped down as Eternal Limited’s chairman of the CSR committee on February 1, 2026, and therefore has not been included in the FY26 list of the CSR committee.The CSR committee meets at least once a year to review and guide Eternal’s ESG strategy and monitor plan of action and progress. At the executive level, the Chief Sustainability Officer maintains oversight on matters related to ESG including climate, supported by the ESG team. The ESG team reports achievements and progress to the CSR committee comprising members of Eternal’s Board of Directors.\nDisclosure Questions Policy and management processes10.\n          \nDetails of Review of NGRBCs by the Company:  \nSubject for Review\nIndicate whether review was undertaken by Director/ Committee of the Board/ Any other Committee  \n \nFrequency \n(Annually/ Half yearly/ Quarterly/ Any \nother – please specify)  \nP1\nP2\nP3\nP4\nP5\nP6\nP7\nP8\nP9\nFor P1, P2, P3, P4, P5, P6, P7, P8 and P9\nPerformance against above policies and  follow up action \nCommit   -tee of the Board\nCommit   -tee of the Board\nCommit   -tee of the Board\nCommit   -tee of the Board\nCommit   -tee of the Board\nCommit   -tee of the Board\nCommit   -tee of the Board\nCommit   -tee of the Board\nCommit   -tee of the Board\nAnnually\nCompliance with statutory requirements of relevance to the principles, and, rectification of any non-compliances\nCommit   -tee of the Board\nCommit   -tee of the Board\nCommit   -tee of the Board\nCommit   -tee of the Board\nCommit   -tee of the Board\nCommit   -tee of the Board\nCommit   -tee of the Board\nCommit   -tee of the Board\nCommit   -tee of the Board\nQuarterly\n\n107\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nDisclosure Questions\nP1\nP2\nP3\nP4\nP5\nP6\nP7\nP8\nP9\n11.\n      \nHas the entity carried out independent assessment/ evaluation of the working of its policies by an external agency? (Yes/ No). If yes, provide the name of the agency.\nY \nN*\nY** - DNV-