 consumables.\n2.  Details of retirement benefits, for Current F and Previous F:\nBenefitsFY 2025-26FY 2024-25\nNo. of \nemployees \ncovered as \na % of total \nemployees\nNo. of \nworkers \ncovered as \na % of total \nworkersȣ\nDeducted and \ndeposited \nwith the \nauthority \n(Y/N/ N.A.)\nNo. of \nemployees \ncovered as \na % of total \nemployees\nNo. of \nworkers \ncovered as \na % of total \nworkers\nDeducted and \ndeposited \nwith the \nauthority \n(Y/N/ N.A.)\nPF**99.73%99.82%Y***99.58%99.73%Y\nGratuity100%100%N.A.****100%100%N.A.\nESI*****100%100%Y100%100%Y\nOthers – \nNPS******\n100%-Y100%-Y\n    Note: In FY26, Eternal’s coverage of the above disclosure consists of permanent employees, other than permanent employees and \nother than permanent workers. \n    *T\nhe reported figures for Provident Fund (PF), Gratuity and ESI coverage, of other than permanent workers, are based on \nconfirmations received from third party vendors.\n    **The above percentage represents data for eligible employees and workers as at the end of the financial year. All eligible employees \nget PF benefits. However, the reported data is less than 100% since some employees have voluntarily opted out of PF contribution. \nFor workers, the reported data is less than 100% since one worker has voluntarily opted out of PF contribution. \n    ***The deduction and deposition with the authority of PF for ‘Other than permanent workers’ is ensured by their respective employers \n(third party vendors).\n    **\n**Eternal has funded the gratuity liability as on 31\nst\n March 2026 upto 94% of the total gratuity liability with ICICI Prudential Life \nInsurance Company Limited.\n    *****The above percentage of ESI represents data for eligible employees and workers at the end of the financial year. Out of the \ntotal headcount, 65 employees and 425 workers (as on 31\nst\n March 2026) and 144 employees and 309 workers (as on 31\nst\n  March 2025) \nwere eligible for ESI.\n    **\n****The above percentage of NPS represents data for eligible employees at the end of the financial year. Out of the total headcount, \n110 employees had opted for the National Pension Scheme as on 31\nst\n March 2026.\n3.  Accessibility of workplaces \n                  Are the premises/ offices of the entity accessible to differently-abled employees and workers, as per \nthe requirements of the Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016ȟ If not, whether any steps are \nbeing taken by the entity in this regard.\n    Our headquarters and largest office based in Gurugram underwent a third party accessibility audit in \nFY24. We have implemented various measures such as ramps/ tactile pathways, disability-friendly \nelevators, gender-neutral accessible toilets and accessible parking at our Gurugram office. Additionally, \nall new facilities under our direct lease have been evaluated for accessibility standards outlined in the \nRPwD Act, 2016.\n4.     Does the entity have an equal opportunity policy as per the Rights of Persons with Disabilities \nAct, 2016? If so, provide a web link to the policy. \n    Yes. Eternal has an Equal opportunity, diversity and inclusion policy aligned with the principles of Rights \nof Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016. (link to the policy) \n\n116\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\n5.       Return  to  work  and  Retention  rates  of  permanent  employees  and  workers  that  took  \nparental leave in FY26: \nGenderPermanent employees Permanent workers\nReturn to \nwork rate\nRetention \nrate\nReturn to \nwork rate\nRetention \nrate\nMale98.17%63.50%--\nFemale96.88%56.76%--\nOthers----\nTo t a l98.01%   62.07%--\n6.     Is there a mechanism available to receive and redress grievances for the following categories \nof employees and workers? If yes, give details of the mechanism in brief. \nYes/ No (If Yes, then give details of the mechanism in brief) \nPermanent workersNot applicable\nOther than permanent workers\nYes. Eternal has established a grievance redressal mechanism for \nits workforce to receive and address their concerns.\nEternal has a dedicated email address accessible for all to raise \nany complaints/ issues. Complaints can be filed by writing to the \nPeople assist team at peopleassist@eternal.com, the Internal \nCommittee at speakup@eternal.com (for sexual harassment \nrelated complaints) or hr@eternal.com (for human rights related \ncomplaints).\n(Stakeholder management policy and Equal opportunity, diversity \nand inclusion policy) \nPermanent employees\nOther than permanent employees\n7.  Membership of employees and workers in association(s) or Unions recognized by the listed entity:\nCategoryFY 2025-26FY 2024-25\nTotal \nemployees/\nworkers \nin the \nrespective \ncategory \n(A)\n No. of employees/\nworkers in the \nrespective \ncategory, who \nare part of the \nassociation(s) or \nUnion (B)\n%  \n(B/A) \n \n \n \n \nTotal \nemployees/\nworkers \nin the \nrespective \ncategory \n(A)\n No. of employees/\nworkers in the \nrespective \ncategory, who \nare part of the \nassociation(s) or \nUnion (B)\n%  \n(B/A) \n \n \n \n \nTotal permanent \nemployees\n6,750--    6,903-   -\nMale4,761--    4,895-   -\nFemale1,987--    2,007-   -\nOther2--1-   -\nTotal permanent \nworkers\n0--0-   -\nMale0--0-   -\nFemale0--0-   -\nOther--   ---   -\n\n117\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\n8.  Details of training given to employees and workers: \nCategoryFY 2025-26FY 2024-25\nTotal \n(A) \n \nOn health and \nsafety measures \nOn skill \nupgradation\nTotal \n(D) \n \nOn health and \nsafety measures \nOn skill \nupgradation\nNo. (B)% (B/A)No. (C)% (C/A)No. (E)% (E/D) No.(F) % (F/D)\nEmployeesȣ\nMale4,761   4,650   97.67%4,665   97.98%4,895 4,68495.69%4,431   90.52%\nFemale1,987 1,95498.34%1,951   98.19%2,007   1,937   96.51%1,790   89.19%\nOthers2   2100%2  100%1   1100%1  100%\nTo t a l6,750   6,606   97.87%6,618  98.04%6,903   6,622   95.93%6,222  90.13%\nWorkersȣȣ\nMale451   434  96.23%--300   287  95.67%-    -\nFemale93   90 96.77%--78   77 98.72%-    -\nOthers0   0   0%--0   00%-    -\nTo t a l544   524  96.32%--378   364  96.30%-    -\n*In FY26, Eternal’s coverage of the above disclosure consists of health & safety and skill upgradation training provided to permanent \nemployees.\n**In FY26, Eternal’s coverage of the above disclosure consists of health & safety training provided to other than permanent workers.\n9.  Details of performance and career development reviews of employees and workers:\nCategoryF 2025-26ȣFY 2024-25\nTotal (A)No. (B)% (B/A)Total (C)No. (D)% (D/C)\nEmployees\nMale4,761   4,761100%4,895  4,895100%\nFemale1,987   1,987100%2,007  2,007100%\nOthers22100%11100%\nTo t a l6,750   6,750100%6,903  6,903100%\nWorkers\nMale------\nFemale------\nOthers------\nTo t a l------\n    *The above disclosure is based on all permanent employees who were active in the performance review cycle, representing 100% \ncoverage of the applicable employee population for performance reviews. \n\n118\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\n10.  Health and safety management system: \n a.     Whether an occupational health and safety management system has been implemented by \nthe entity? (Yes/ No). If yes, the coverage of such a system? \n       Yes. At Eternal, our Health and Safety Policy includes nine pillars that outline how we effectively manage \ncritical Occupational Health and Safety (OH&S) aspects. Additionally, we hold the ISO 45001:2018 \ncertification for our OH&S Management System. The Audit Committee of Eternal Limited maintains \noversight on health and safety related matters and reviews them on a quarterly basis.\n \n      The Environment, Health and Safety (EHS) team helps the organization maintain high levels of safety \nstandards. Our offices are equipped with essential safety systems such as fire detection, fire fighting \nmechanisms, public address systems, safety signage, etc., to ensure the safety of employees and \nworkers. Additionally, there is a Safety Reporting System (SRS) that enables employees and workers \nto report OH&S incidents and hazards actively. The system ensures seamless tracking, analysis and \nresolution, thereby building a proactive safety culture and enabling continuous improvement of our \nOH&S management system. \n     Health and Safety training is mandatory for all employees of Eternal and a key pillar of our Health & \nSafety policy. In FY26, we strengthened our safety culture through a series of focused awareness \ninitiatives. We marked National Road Safety month, with a mix of on-ground and digital engagement \nactivities, including interactive carnivals, recognition of road safety champions under the company-\nwide “Sadak Suraksha Jeevan Raksha” campaign. During this month, we also conducted engaging \nawareness initiatives across offices, emphasizing people safety and SRS reporting, while reinforcing \nthe message that “safety is a habit we build together.” These efforts are further supported by \ncontinuous monitoring by the Workplace & Facilities team to ensure sustained adherence to safety \nstandards.\n  b.         What  are  the  processes  used  to  identify  work-related  hazards  and  assess  risks  on  a  routine  and  \nnon-routine basis by the entity?\n     Risk identification and mitigation remain integral to our Health and Safety policy.  We adopt a proactive \napproach to identifying, assessing and mitigating hazards across both routine and non-routine \nactivities. An integrated action plan is also in place to effectively respond to emergency situations.\n     As part of our ISO 45001:2018-certified Occupational Health and Safety Management System, we \nfollow documented procedures to systematically evaluate work-related hazards and risks across all \noperations. Employees and workers are encouraged to report hazards, near-misses and incidents \nthrough our Safety Reporting System (SRS), enabling structured investigation and timely closure of \nsuch cases.\n     We continuously strengthen our approach through regular internal inspections and audits to \nproactively identify and address workplace risks. Identified risks are managed through structured \naction plans with defined timelines and measurable targets to ensure effective mitigation.\n  c.\n  \n      Wh\nether  you  have  processes  for  workers  to  report  the  work-related  hazards  and  to  remove  \nthemselves from such risks. (Y/ N)\n    \n \nYe\ns, we have established a robust process for workers to report work-related hazards and protect \nthemselves from associated risks. In FY24, we introduced the Safety Reporting System (SRS), enabling \nemployees and workers to report health and safety hazards, incidents, and near-misses.\n    \n \nBu\nilding on this, the process is now well established across the organization. During FY26, we further \nstrengthened its implementation through multiple awareness sessions, townhalls and training \nprograms, ensuring it remains a reliable and accessible platform. We also ensured timely closure of \nidentified risks, supported by effective Root Cause Analysis (RCA) and implementation of Corrective \nand Preventive Actions (CAPA).\n\n119\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\n       As part of our annual safety campaigns, we launched the “7 Habits of Eternal Safety Champions” \nin FY25, reinforcing that safety is a shared responsibility built through consistent behaviors and \ncollective commitment. This is a successful ongoing campaign in FY26. This initiative has significantly \nstrengthened safety awareness, encouraged proactive hazard identification and inculcated a culture \nof shared responsibility across the organization.\n d.      Do the employees/ workers of the entity have access to non-occupational medical and healthcare \nservices?  (Yes/ No)\n    \n \nYe\ns. Eternal provides its employees with non-occupational medical and healthcare benefits. These \ninclude comprehensive group medical insurance that extends to the employee, spouse/ partner and \nup to four children. The medical insurance has progressive coverages such as treatment for autism \nand psychiatric ailments, lifelong coverage for children living with disabilities, etc. We also offer free \nonline medical consultations, nutrition counselling, psychological counselling, discounted diagnostic \ntests and discounts on medicines, etc.\n     Over and above, all employees are covered under the group accidental insurance. We also provide \nmedical insurance or ESI benefits to our other than permanent workers basis applicability.\n11.  Details of safety related incidents, in the following format: \nSafety incident/ numberCategoryȣFY 2025-26FY 2024-25\nLost Time Injury Frequency Rate (LTIFR) (per one-\nmillion-person hour worked)\nEmployees00\nWorkers  0 0\nTotal recordable work-related injuriesEmployees00\nWorkers00\nNo. of fatalities Employees00\nWorkers00\nHigh consequence work-related injury or ill-\nhealth (excluding fatalities)\nEmployees00\nWorkers00\n *Including in the contract workforce \n12.  Describe the measures taken by the entity to ensure a safe and healthy workplace. \n    At Eternal, we prioritize the safety and welfare of our employees and their overall well-being. We have \nimplemented comprehensive policies and protocols aimed at fostering an incident-free, vibrant and \nsecure workplace environment. We have dedicated teams which are responsible for monitoring safety \nperformance and implementing necessary measures to enhance safety protocols. The following outline \nseveral initiatives designed to promote a culture of health, safety and wellbeing of employees within our \norganization:\n  Imp\nlemented Occupational Health & Safety Management System (ISO 45001:2018) \n  Medical rooms with dedicated doctors and nurses in our corporate office\n    Fire detection, fire fighting equipment and first-aid facilities active in our offices to ensure prompt \nresponse and mitigation during incidents\n    Imp\nlemented measures for preparing and responding to fire emergencies and conducting regular \nmock drills to assess our readiness and improve our response capabilities\n  Aw\nareness sessions promoting healthy postures\n  Us\ne of Personal Protective Equipment (PPE) by the workforce \n\n120\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\n    In the event of serious injuries, investigations are conducted to prevent recurrence. Corrective and \nPreventive Actions (CAPA) are diligently implemented following investigations\n  Group health insurance for employees and dependents\n  Group personal accident insurance\n  Di\nscounts on diagnostic services\n  Free telehealth and medical consultation for employees and dependents\n  Di\nscounted dental consultations and free vision checkups\n  Eq\nual paid parental leave of 26 weeks provided to mothers and fathers\n   We\nllness leaves (physical and mental), period leaves and paid annual leaves provided\n  Mental wellness expert counselling by the in-house team of trained psychologists and counsellors\n  Nutrition counselling\n  Gym facilities across multiple offices\n    Regular campaigns and training conducted on health and safety topics such as First Aid, CPR, \nfirefighting and evacuation procedures\n    Ma\nndatory EHS training and assessment implemented for all employees via Eternal Learning Hub (ELH \nplatform)\n13. Number of complaints on the following made by employees and workers: \nFY 2025-26FY 2024-25\nFiled \nduring \nthe year\nPending \nresolution at \nthe end of year\nRemarks \n \nFiled \nduring \nthe year\nPending \nresolution at \nthe end of year\nRemarks \n \nWorking conditions*00-130-\nHealth and safety**10-00-\n    *In FY26, we continued to prioritize open communication and proactive resolution through our robust working conditions complaint \nmanagement process. We have a dedicated team that ensures instant resolution of complaints (temperature, ergonomics, leakage, \nwashroom hygiene, etc.) to provide a safe & healthy working environment, however for the above indicator we report only high risk \ncomplaints based on internal SOP.\n    **We have a dedicated Safety Reporting System (SRS) for all employees to report Health & Safety issues. The EHS team follows an \ninternal SOP to validate and resolve all complaints received on SRS in a stipulated time.\n14.  Assessments for the year:\n% of your plants and offices that were assessed  \n(by entity or statutory authorities or third parties)\nHealth and safety practices100%\nAn independent Human Rights Assessment (including Health & Safety) \nwas conducted for all our office locations. In addition, we continue \nto hold ISO 45001:2018 certification for our Gurugram and Bengaluru \noffices.\nWorking conditions\n\n121\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\n15.    Provide details of any corrective action taken or underway to address safety-related incidents \n(if any) and on significant risks/ concerns arising from assessments of health & safety practices \nand working conditions. \n    We have conducted comprehensive risk assessments and revised risk evaluations across all activities in \nour offices to ensure continued compliance with safety standards and best practices. Through our internal \nassessment we have confirmed that our operations align with the established safety protocols since there \nwere zero safety-related incidents reported in FY26 (Principle 3, Q11), therefore no immediate corrective \nactions or mitigation measures were required.\n    We hold ISO 45001:2018 certification for our Gurugram and Bengaluru offices. For our newly leased \noffices we are in the process of attaining these certifications. Our Occupational Health and Safety (OH&S) \nmanagement system remains aligned with the requirements of the standard, reaffirming the robustness \nof the framework.\n    In addition, an independent Human Rights assessment has been conducted for all our office locations and \nno major findings were reported during the financial year. \nLeadership Indicators\n1.      Does the entity extend any life insurance or any compensatory package in the event of death of \n(A) Employees (Y/ N) (B) Workers (Y/ N). \n •   Employees: Yes, all employees are covered by a comprehensive group accident insurance policy that \nprovides compensation to their families in the unfortunate event of the death of an employee due to \nan accident.\n     The company also provides a generous ex gratia payment to the families of all our deceased employees. \nThis payment amounts to the employee’s annual salary and continues for a duration of 10 years from \nthe date of the employee’s passing.\n         •  Workers: Not applicable.\n5. Details on assessment of value chain partners:\n% of value chain partners (by value of business done with such \npartners) that were assessedȣ\nHealth and safety practices 16.95% by total spend on suppliers (upstream)**\n5.08% by total revenue from restaurant partners (downstream)***\nWorking Conditions\n *Value chain disclosures are based on the previous financial year (FY25).\n    **The assessment was conducted for 50 upstream suppliers. During the year, we significantly expanded the coverage of supplier \nassessment compared to the previous year. While the percentage based on spend reflects changes in procurement mix and supplier \nspend distribution within the year, the program now covers a broader set of value chain partners.\n ***The assessment was conducted for 8 downstream customers.\n\n122\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nPrinciple 4 – Businesses should respect the interests of and be responsive to all its stakeholders\nEssential Indicators\n1.  Describe the processes for identifying key stakeholder groups of the entity. \n    In our Stakeholder management policy, which is in line with the AA1000 Stakeholder Engagement \nStandard, we have analyzed our internal and external environment to identify stakeholders including \nindividuals, groups of individuals or organizations:\n •   Who are directly or indirectly dependent on our activities, products or services and associated \nperformance, whom we are dependent on in order to operate effectively\n •   To \nwhom Eternal has, or in the future may have, legal, commercial, operational or ethical/ moral \nresponsibilities\n • Wh\no can influence or have impact on Eternal’s strategic or operational decision-making\n Based on the above definition, we have identified our key stakeholders as follows:\n • Cu\nstomers (end users, restaurant partners and business partners)\n • Emp\nloyees\n • Ve\nndors \n • Delivery partners\n • Sha\nreholders \n • Government and regulators\n • Com\nmunity/ society\n (l\nink to the policy)\n2.    List stakeholder groups identified as key for your entity and the frequency of engagement with \neach stakeholder group. \n    At Eternal, stakeholder engagement is done through multiple channels. We have developed engagement \nprograms that are applicable across all local operations.\nStakeholder \ngroup\nWhether \nidentified as \nvulnerable & \nmarginalized \ngroup  \n(Yes/ No) \nChannels of communication \n(Email, SMS, Newspaper, \nPamphlets, Advertisement, \nCommunity meetings, \nNotice board, Website), \nOther\nFrequency of \nengagement \n(Annually/ half-\nyearly/ quarterly/ \nothers – please \nspecify)\nPurpose and scope of \nengagement including key \ntopics and concerns raised \nduring such engagement\nEmployees  No• Employee newsletters \n• Intranet portal \n• Employee resource groups \n• Posters, TV screens \n• \n Int\nernal communication \nchannels\n• Employee town halls \n• Employee surveys \n• Performance reviews\n•  De\ndicated email IDs \n(including grievance \nredressal)\n• Notice boards\nOngoing/ \nContinuous\n• Employee benefits \n• \n Aw\nareness about \ncompany policies\n• Employee recognition \n• Learning and development \n•  He\nalth, safety and well-\nbeing\n•  Pe\nrformance review and \ncareer development\n• Grievance redressal\n• Company updates\n• Community connect\n\n123\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nStakeholder \ngroup\nWhether \nidentified as \nvulnerable & \nmarginalized \ngroup  \n(Yes/ No) \nChannels of communication \n(Email, SMS, Newspaper, \nPamphlets, Advertisement, \nCommunity meetings, \nNotice board, Website), \nOther\nFrequency of \nengagement \n(Annually/ half-\nyearly/ quarterly/ \nothers – please \nspecify)\nPurpose and scope of \nengagement including key \ntopics and concerns raised \nduring such engagement\nCustomers \n(end-users)\nNo• \n Zomato app and Eternal’s \nwebsite \n• Customer service support \n• \n Customer satisfaction \nsurvey\n• Email communication\n• \n Online (including social \nmedia) and offline \nmarketing channels\nOngoing/ \nContinuous\n• Customer feedback \n• Grievance redressal\n• Resolution of their queries\n• Advertising\nCustomer \n(Restaurants)\nNo• \n Zomato restaurant partner \napp \n• Zomato dashboard \n• Dedicated email ID\n• Account managers\n• Social media channels\nOngoing/ \nContinuous\n• Onboarding support\n• Resolving queries\n• Grievance redressal\n• \n Discussions on ensuring \nsafe deliveries\n•  Enhancing user \nexperience\n• Capturing feedback\n•  Creating and updating \nmenu, pricing, \npromotions, etc.\n•  Understanding and \nactivating growth levers - \ne.g. advertising\nCustomers \n(Business \npartners)\nNo• Dedicated email IDOngoing/ \nContinuous\n• Customer feedback \n• Resolving queries\n• Grievance redressal\nVendorsNo• Dedicated email ID\n• Vendor evaluation\n• Surveys\n• Telephonic interactions\n• Face-to-face interactions \n• \n Ot\nher electronic modes of \ncommunication\nOngoing/ \nContinuous\n• Resolving queries\n• Assessing performance\n• \n Tr\naining and awareness \n(including upskilling on \nESG parameters)\n•  Sh\naring requests and \nrequirements\n\n124\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nStakeholder \ngroup\nWhether \nidentified as \nvulnerable & \nmarginalized \ngroup  \n(Yes/ No) \nChannels of communication \n(Email, SMS, Newspaper, \nPamphlets, Advertisement, \nCommunity meetings, \nNotice board, Website), \nOther\nFrequency of \nengagement \n(Annually/ half-\nyearly/ quarterly/ \nothers – please \nspecify)\nPurpose and scope of \nengagement including key \ntopics and concerns raised \nduring such engagement\nDelivery \npartners \n(DPs)\nNo*• \n Zomato delivery partner \napp \n• Dedicated email IDs \n• \n Hotline number for SOS \ncalls from delivery partners \n• \n Zonal Head/ Fleet \noperations managers \n•  Zone-wise dedicated \nWhatsApp channels\nOngoing/ \nContinuous\n•  Seamless onboarding \nsupport through \nWhatsApp and other \nchannels\n• \n Efficient & timely query \nresolution\n•  Effective grievance \nredressal\n• Safety and well-being \n• Skill based trainings\n• \n Fe\nedback driven process \nimprovement\n• \n Transparent earnings and \npayout management\n•  “Rent an EV” feature for \neasy rentals\nShareholders No•  Shareholder/ investor \nmeetings\n•  Shareholders’ letters and \nearnings calls\n• Newspaper advertisements\n• Email communication\n• Annual Report\n• Stock exchange filings\nPeriodic basis• \n Queries on financial and \noperational performance \nof the company\n•  Transparent disclosures of \nmaterial events\n•  Other topics relevant to \nshareholders\nCommunity/ \nSociety\nYes*Channels and \ncommunication with \nNGOs, Government bodies, \nadvocacy groups and PwDs:\n• \n Consultations and \nmeetings\n• \n Email and digital \ncommunication\n• \n Ro\nundtable discussions \nand events\n• \n Co\nllaborative workshops \nand listening sessions\nOngoing/\nContinuous\n• Skill based training \n•  Enabling access to \nlivelihood\n•  Su\npport in attaining \nassistive devices \n• Safety and wellbeing \nGovernment \nand \nregulators\nNo• \n Pu\nblic policy submissions \nand representations \n• Formal dialogues \nOn need basis  •  Inp\nuts on draft regulations \nand policies that have \nbearing on our operations \nand businesses\n*Selected members of our delivery partner community and selected members of society fall under the definition of vulnerable and \nmarginalized groups, such as Persons with Disabilities and women from economically-weaker sections. \n\n125\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nLeadership Indicators\n1.         Provide  the  processes  for  consultation  between  stakeholders  and  the  board  on  economic,  \nenvironmental  and  social  topics  or  if  consultation  is  delegated,  how  is  feedback  from  such  \nconsultations provided to the Board. \n    We regularly engage with our key stakeholders (end-users, restaurant partners and delivery partners) \nas outlined in our Stakeholder management policy. Each business unit in every operating location is \nrequired to carry out stakeholder engagement in line with the stakeholder management policy. Through \nthe channels described in the policy, the functional teams in Eternal receive feedback from various groups \nof stakeholders (external and internal) on our initiatives and operations. This feedback is analyzed and \ndiscussed with relevant leadership teams to identify, develop and implement the environmental and \nsocial topics. Select actions are discussed with the CSR committee of the board based on their overall \nimportance and these meetings are minuted. The CSR Committee at Eternal also undertakes responsibility \nfor climate and sustainability-related matters.\n (link to the policy)\n2.    Whether stakeholder consultation is used to support the identification and management of \nenvironmental,  and  social  topics  (Yes/  No).  If  so,  provide  details  of  instances  as  to  how  the  \ninputs  received  from  stakeholders  on  these  topics  were  incorporated  into  the  policies  and  \nactivities of the entity. \n    Yes. Stakeholder consultation was an integral part of the double materiality assessment that we conducted \nin FY26 with the help of an independent external organization to refresh the set of ESG topics material to \nthe firm. Additionally, we regularly engage with local stakeholders via various channels to identify their \nemerging concerns to make improvements and design innovative offerings to meet their expectations. \nWe also conduct regular surveys and impact assessments of projects that involve local stakeholders.\n    Below are the examples of how Eternal has used inputs received from stakeholders on material \nsustainability issues to design new initiatives and offerings in FY26:\nStakeholder groupInput modesIncorporation of inputs\nEmployees   Online channels \n(slack, emails, etc.)\n\n   Discussions with \nemployees across \nforums and targeted \nsession\nMaterial topic: Climate action and circular economy\nBased on input received from employees, one of the \ninitiatives the company launched in FY26 was an \ninternal sustainability education program including:\n\n  Regular learning sessions with external and internal \nexperts. In FY26, we conducted sessions focused on \nafforestation and water conservation.\n\n  A de\ndicated online channel and community for \nenvironmental education, exchange of ideas and \nefforts made by employees towards environmental \nprotection.\n\n        Oũ\nine activities to raise awareness among \nemployees on sustainable packaging, repurposing \nwaste  and to encourage the adoption of sustainable \npractices, such as the appropriate segregation and \ndisposal of various kinds of waste. \n\n126\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nStakeholder groupInput modesIncorporation of inputs\nCustomers  \n(End-users)\nFeedback received via \na range of customer \ncommunication \nchannels \nMaterial topic: Climate action and circular economy\n\n  Zomato Plastic-Free Future Program: In response \nto customer concerns around receiving food from \nrestaurants in plastic packaging, we launched the \n‘Zomato Plastic-Free Future Program’ in December \n2024. The program recognizes restaurant partners \nactively adopting sustainable alternatives to \nplastic packaging for food deliveries. More than \n350 restaurant brands, representing 10,000+ \nrestaurants across 500+ cities listed on the Zomato \napp have already joined the program by submitting \ndetailed evidence of their packaging, including \nphotos and certificates, confirming that their \nmain menu items are being packed in sustainable \nalternatives. To recognize their efforts, these \nrestaurants now carry a prominent ‘Low plastic \npackaging’ tag on their menu pages, banners and a \nclear label in the order summary, making it easy for \ncustomers to identify these restaurants. Customers \ncan also discover these restaurants faster by typing \n‘Low plastic packaging’ in the search bar or using the \n‘Low plastic packaging’ filter option. As of March 31, \n2026, we have facilitated 100 million food delivery \norders through restaurants on the Zomato Plastic-\nFree Future Program. \nMaterial topic: Innovation\n\n        Healthy mode: To capture the growing demand for \nhealthier meals, Eternal launched “Healthy mode” \non the Zomato app in top 7 cities in FY26. With this \nmode, end users see a curation of  restaurants along \nwith complete nutritional information, i.e. macros, \ningredient-level description and customization \nfilters. Since its launch, Healthy Mode has helped \ncustomers make more informed and healthier food \nchoices, generating ~8.5 million orders in FY26.\nDelivery partners\n   In-\nperson interviews\n\n   Surveys (including \nDelivery partner \nwellbeing survey)\n\n   Focus Group \nDiscussions (FGDs) \nMaterial topic: Stakeholder value creation\n        Social & Financial security through Government \nscheme facilitation: In FY26, in response to \nrequests for support from our delivery partners, \nEternal designed and launched a structured \nprogram to facilitate their better access to central \nand state government schemes. This initiative was \ndesigned to address key barriers faced by partners \nin accessing schemes including lack of awareness, \ntime scarcity, documentation challenges and \nunclear requirements. \n   Th\ne program, run in collaboration with the social \nenterprise, Haqdarshak ran through a series of \non-ground facilitation camps and digital support \nmechanisms across India. In FY26, over 5,500 \nZomato delivery partners were enrolled in at least \none government scheme, unlocking benefits valued \nat over INR 120 crore. These benefits span critical \nareas such as healthcare, insurance and income \nsupport. \n\n127\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nStakeholder groupInput modesIncorporation of inputs\nCommunity/ \nSociety\n   In-person & online \ndiscussions\n Feedback form\nMaterial topic: Stakeholder value creation\n        Improvements in app accessibility for Persons \nwith Disabilities (PwD) - Based on feedback from \nthe community of Persons with Disabilities, in \nFY26 we launched a feature on the Zomato food \ndelivery app, that enables customers to disclose \ntheir mobility, vision and hearing needs to receive \na customized platform and delivery experience. \nAdditionally, select members of Eternal’s tech, \nproduct and design teams underwent the \nIAAP CPACC certification in accessibility. The \ncertification verifies a professional’s broad, cross-\ndisciplinary knowledge of disabilities, universal \ndesign and global accessibility laws. Completion of \nthis training empowers team members to practically \napply accessibility principles in the course of \nproduct development. \n3.     Provide details of instances of engagement with and actions are taken to, address the concerns \nof vulnerable/ marginalized stakeholder groups. \n    Eternal has identified vulnerable/ marginalized stakeholders groups both within and outside of its value \nchain and has taken multiple actions to address their concerns.\n    Below are the actions taken to address concerns of vulnerable/ marginalized groups within Eternal’s value \nchain:\n    Zo\nmato’s  Equitable  Action  for  Livelihood  (ZEAL)  program- Launched in FY23, our PwD delivery \npartner initiative has benefitted over 6,000 PwDs with a range of disabilities including locomotor \nand hearing and speech impairment. In FY26 alone 3,500+ PwD DPs, collectively delivered over 10 \nmillion orders and covered over 40 million kms. The program benefits include waiver of onboarding \nfee, assignment of short-distance deliveries only, enhanced payouts, sensitized fleet coaches and \nsupport teams to better assist delivery partners as needed. In addition, restaurant partners and end-\nusers are notified when a PwD delivery partner is assigned to an order and requested to extend their \nsupport.\n               Zomato’s  Women  riding  centers-  Facilitates two-wheeler riding training for women, basic digital \nliteracy and confidence building. Through this initiative, Eternal aims to empower women by enhancing \ntheir mobility, enabling them to meet their personal needs with greater independence while also \nunlocking access to livelihood opportunities. Highlighted below are the benefits offered at these \ncenters:\n                    A d\nedicated space for women and their families to meet with company representatives, inquire \nand seek guidance on working in the gig economy. \n                   A safe space for women to connect, support each other, and build a sense of community.\n                    Guidance to test and understand the available EV-bike options for interested participants.\n Other initiatives to ensure women safety and support:\n                24ɐ\n7 SOS emergency support: Available in 900+ cities, the emergency response system connects \ndelivery partners to support like ambulance, police and an in-house response team.\n                 Ad\ndress visibility at night: Between 7 PM and 5 AM, women delivery partners have the option to avoid \nlocations where they may feel unsafe.\n\n128\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\n                 City-specific chat support groups: All women delivery partners in the top seven cities are added to \nWhatsApp support groups for seamless communication, grievance redressal and peer support.\n   Hotel delivery protocol: Women delivery partners delivering to hotels, lodges or guest houses can \ncomplete deliveries at the reception instead of going to rooms.\n    Black  zones  reporting: Delivery partners can report unsafe or difficult-to-navigate areas through \nthe delivery partner app. More than 270 black zones were identified as of 31\nst\n March 2026, with such \nlocations marked unserviceable, where required, to enhance safety.\n Actions taken to address the concerns of vulnerable/marginalized groups outside of the value chain:\n    Eternal apps allow customers to contribute to Feeding India, a not-for-profit initiative, directly at the \ncheckout page. These contributions support Feeding India’s mission, focused on eradicating hunger \nand malnutrition among children from under-served communities across the country. Through Feeding \nIndia, 1.4 lakh+ children now receive nourishing meals daily across 2,300+ centers in 150+ cities. In \nFY26, we deepened this work by revamping 45 Anganwadis across Varanasi, Nandurbar, Kushinagar \nand Gurugram. Since its inception, Feeding India has served over 290 million meals till FY26. We also \ndeployed facial recognition technology across educational centers to track meal distribution digitally, \nensuring both accountability and scale without adding administrative burden.\n Fo\nr further details of key programs and initiatives, please refer to the Feeding India Annual Report FY25\nPrinciple 5 – Businesses should respect and promote human rights\nEssential Indicators\n1.      Employees and workers who have been provided training on human rights issues and policy(ies) \nof the entity, in the following format: \nCategoryFY 2025-26FY 2024-25\nTotal \n(A) \nNo. of employees \n/ workers \ncovered (B)\n% \n(B/A) \nTotal \n(A) \nNo. of employees \n/ workers \ncovered (B)\n% \n(B/A)  \nEmployeesȣ\nPermanent6,7506,515  96.52%6,9036,623   95.94%\nOther than permanent245240  97.96%503371  73.76%\nTotal employees6,9956,755   96.57%7,4066,994   94.44%\nWorkers \nPermanent00   0%00   0%\nOther than permanent \nworkers**\n544524 96.32%378364  96.30%\nTotal workers 544524 96.32%378364  96.30%\n    *For employees, training on Human rights has been conducted online via our internal learning platform. Additionally, we also \ncirculated posters and videos via our internal channel. POSH training has been conducted at the time of induction. \n **For other than permanent workers, Human rights training has been conducted in-person via videos.\n\n129\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\n2.  Details of minimum wages paid to employees and workers, in the following formatȣ: \nCategoryFY 2025-26FY 2024-25\nTotal \n(A) \n \nEqual to \nminimum wage \nMore than \nminimum wage \nTotal \n(D) \n \nEqual to \nminimum wage \nMore than \nminimum wage \nNo. (B)% (B/A)No. (C)% (C/A)No. (E)% (E/D)No. (F)% (F/D)\nEmployees\nPermanent6,7500   0%6,750100%6,9030   0% 6,903100%\nMale4,7610   0%4,761100%4,8950   0% 4,895100%\nFemale1,9870   0%1,987100%2,0070   0% 2,007100%\nOther2   0   0%   2100%1   00%1  100%\nOther than \npermanent\n245   0   0%  245100%5030   0%  503   100%\nMale2030   0%203   100%4090   0%  409  100%\nFemale420   0%42  100%930   0%93  100%\nOther0   0   0%   0   0%1   00%1  100%\nWorkers \nPermanent0   0   0%   0   0%0   00%0   0%\nMale0   0   0%   0   0%0   00%0   0%\nFemale0   0   0%   0   0%0   00%0   0%\nOthers0   0   0%   0   0%0   00%0   0%\n