lease specify level of treatment00\nTotal water discharged (in kilolitres)761.071,040.95\n    *Environmental performance data for this indicator covers Bengaluru office only based on Eternal’s boundary determined by \noperational control approach. Water discharge values are calculated based on an assumption that 80% of the water supply flows \nback into the ecosystem (through sewage system) as wastewater (as referenced in the NITI Aayog report).\n  Note : Indicate if any independent assessment/ evaluation/ assurance has been carried out by an external \nagency? (Y/ N) If yes, name of the external agency. \n Yes, assured by Deloitte Haskins & Sells LLP for FY26.\n5.     Has the entity implemented a mechanism for Zero Liquid Discharge? If yes, provide details of \nits coverage and implementation. \n    Yes, our corporate offices in Gurugram (multi-tenant facilities), has zero liquid discharge mechanism. \nWe do not have operational control over our Gurugram offices. For all our other offices, wastewater \nis discharged into municipal sewers, which undergo further treatment in line with applicable \nrequirements.\n\n136\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\n6.     Please provide details of air emissions (other than GHG emissions) by the entity, in the following \nformat:\nParameterUnitFY 2025-26FY 2024-25\nNOxMT0.0005044980.000690665\nSOxMT0.0000997760.000133844\nParticulate matter (PM)MT0.0001827400.000231416\nPersistent organic pollutants (POP)MT--\nVolatile organic compounds (VOC)MT--\nHazardous air pollutants (HAP)MT--\nOthers - Hydrocarbon (HC)MT0.000090820-\nOthers - Carbon Monoxide (CO)MT0.000423773-\n    Note:    Indicate  if  any  independent    assessment/  evaluation/  assurance  has    been  carried  out  by  an  \nexternal agency? (Y/ N) If yes, name of the external agency. \n Yes, assessment was conducted by Eko Pro Engineers Pvt. Ltd.\n7.        Provide  details  of  greenhouse  gas  emissions  (Scope  1  and  Scope  2  emissions)  and  its  \nintensity, in the following format: \nParameterUnitFY 2025-26FY 2024-25\nTotal Scope 1 emissions (Break-up of the GHG \ninto CO\n2\n, CH4, N2O, HFCs, PFCs, SF6, NF3, if \navailable)*\nMetric \ntonnes of CO\n2\n \nequivalent\nGross 25.62 tCO\n2\ne\nNet 0**\nGross 57.27 tCO\n2\ne\nNet 0**\nTotal Scope 2 emissions (Break-up of the GHG \ninto CO\n2\n, CH4, N2O, HFCs, PFCs, SF6, NF3, if \navailable)*\nMetric \ntonnes of CO\n2\n \nequivalent\nGross 70.91 tCO\n2\ne\nNet 0**\nGross 90.42 tCO\n2\ne\nNet 0**\nTotal Scope 1 and Scope 2 emission intensity \nper rupee of turnover (Total Scope 1 and \nScope 2 GHG emissions/ Revenue from \noperations)\nMetric \ntonnes of CO\n2\n \nequivalent\n0.0000000008857 \ntCO\n2\ne/ Rupee of \nturnover\n0.0000000017139 \ntCO\n2\ne/ Rupee of \nturnover\nTotal Scope 1 and Scope 2 emissions \nintensity per rupee of turnover adjusted for \nPurchasing Power Parity (PPP) ((Total Scope \n1 and Scope 2 GHG emissions/ Revenue from \noperations adjusted for PPP)\nMetric \ntonnes of CO\n2\n \nequivalent/ \nrupee of \nturnover \nadjusted for \nPPP\n0.0000000180144\ntCO\n2\ne/ Rupee of \nturnover adjusted \nfor PPP\n0.0000000354097\n tCO\n2\ne/ Rupee of \nturnover adjusted \nfor PPP\nTotal Scope 1 and Scope 2 emission intensity \nin terms of physical output\n---\nTotal Scope 1 and Scope 2 emission intensity \n(optional) – the relevant metric may be \nselected by the entity\n---\n    *Scope 1 & 2 emissions include emissions from fuel consumption, purchased electricity and fugitive emissions for offices within \nEternal’s boundary determined by operational control approach. Emissions from the offices where operational control is not \nestablished, they are accounted for under applicable Scope 3 categories. Please refer to our boundary note on page 86 of this \nannual report for more details.\n    **For Scope 1, the figures above are reported after deducting the carbon removal offsets purchased to maintain carbon neutrality \nfor Scope 1. The company uses emission factors from the IPCC 2006. For Scope 2, the figures above are reported after deducting \nthe International Renewable Energy Certificates (IRECs) purchased to maintain carbon neutrality. The company uses emission \nfactors from CEA 2025.\n\n137\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\n  Note: Indicate if any independent  assessment/ evaluation/ assurance has been carried out by an external \nagency? (Y/ N) If yes, name of the external agency\n Ye\ns, assured by Deloitte Haskins & Sells LLP for FY26.  \n8.        Does  the  entity  have  any  project  related  to  reducing  greenhouse  gas  emission?  If  Yes,  then  \nprovide details. \n Yes, below are the details of our projects-\n  Project 1: C\narbon market instruments\n \n     We have maintained 100% of our Scope 1 & Scope 2 emissions at zero through use of an equivalent \namount of verified carbon removal offsets and International Renewable Energy Certificates (IRECs).\n  Project 2: Transition to EV based food deliveries \n     Our key climate risk mitigation action is to support delivery partners in transitioning to EV bikes to \nundertake food deliveries. To achieve this, we generate awareness about EVs among delivery partners, \ncollaborate with EV ecosystem players to secure access to EVs on rent and advocate for better EV \nproducts, infrastructure and financing.\n                    E\nV  awareness: In FY26, delivery partners were made aware of the benefits of transitioning to \nEVs through multiple engaging videos on the YouTube channel, EV campaigns launched within the \nZomato delivery partner app and also through notifications. We also organized the second edition \nof EV Bazaar in Bengaluru to educate the delivery partners on the benefits of EVs and to support \ntheir transition. Since its inception, the EV Bazaar initiative has achieved significant outreach, \nengaging over 2,000 delivery partners and more than 20 ecosystem providers across Delhi and \nBengaluru. \n                    E\nV  access: To facilitate easier access to EVs on rent, we have established partnerships with \nEV rental agencies, battery swapping companies and EV-based logistics service providers. We \nlaunched the Zomato EV rental fleet on World Environment Day 2025 in Delhi to enable rental \naccess to quality high-speed EVs at affordable prices for delivery partners. As of March 2026, \nmore than 1,500 EVs have been deployed across Delhi and Chennai under the Zomato EV rental \nfleet initiative.  \n                    E\nV  advocacy: We continuously advocate for better EV products, infrastructure and financing for \ndelivery partners. We are a part of global coalitions on EVs including the Climate Group’s EV100 \ninitiative and the UNEP-supported Deliver-e Coalition. We regularly contribute to stakeholder \nconsultations facilitated by central, state and city-level government on the topic of EVs.\n                    D\nelivery  optimization: Alongside supporting partners in adopting EVs for food delivery, we are \ncontinuously improving operational efficiency through delivery optimization measures that bring \ndown delivery distances and time, thereby reducing energy consumption and its associated \nemissions. In FY26, delivery optimization has resulted in avoidance of 8,100 tCO2e of emissions.\n    \n \nAs \na result of our efforts, our last-mile delivery emissions on a per km basis reduced by 11.77% in \nFY26 when compared to FY22. The total EV-based food deliveries increased by 50% compared to the \nprevious year, totaling 132.3 million orders. This represented 13.5% of total food delivery orders for \nthe same year.\n\n138\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\n9.  Provide details related to waste management by the entity, in the following format: \nParameterFY 2025-26FY 2024-25\nTotal waste generated (in metric tonnes)ȣ\nPlastic waste (A)0.510.68\nE-waste** (B)0.160.94\nBio-medical waste (C)0.130.08\nConstruction and demolition waste (D)--\nBattery waste (E)0.320.00\nRadioactive waste (F)--\nOther Hazardous waste. Please specify, if any. (G)0.030.00\nOther Non-hazardous waste generated (H). Please specify, if any. \n(Break-up by composition i.e. by materials relevant to the sector)\n3.55\n(Wet Waste: 1.09\nPaper & \nCardboard: 2.46)\n4.50\n(Wet Waste: 1.53\nPaper & \nCardboard: 2.97)\nTotal (A+B + C + D + E + F + G + H)4.706.19\nWaste intensity per rupee of turnover  \n(Total waste generated/ Revenue from operations)\n0.00000000004\n MT/ Rupee of \nturnover\n0.0000000001\n MT/ Rupee of \nturnover\nWaste intensity per rupee of turnover adjusted for Purchasing \nPower Parity (PPP) (Total waste generated/ Revenue from \noperations adjusted for PPP)\n0.0000000009 \nMT/ Rupee \nof turnover \nadjusted for PPP \n0.0000000015 \nMT/ Rupee \nof turnover \nadjusted for PPP\nWaste intensity in terms of physical output--\nWaste intensity (optional) – the relevant metric may be selected \nby the entity\n--\n    *Environmental performance data for this indicator covers three offices on direct lease (Gurugram, Bengaluru and Mumbai). \nTherefore,  its  disclosure  in  different  waste  categories  is  made  as  per  Eternal’s  boundary  determined  by  operational  control  \napproach. Please refer to our boundary note on page 86 of this annual report for more details.\n **The e-waste generation is based on the e-waste disposal certificates received.\n    For  each  category  of  waste  generated,  total  waste  recovered  through  recycling,  re-using  or  other  \nrecovery operations (in metric tonnes)ȣ\nFY 2025-26FY 2024-25\nCategory of waste:\n(i)  Recycled4.654.59\n(ii)  Re-used0.000.00\n(iii) \n Other recovery operations0.000.00\nTo t a l   4.654.59\n\n139\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\n  For each category of waste generated, total waste disposed of by nature of disposal method (in metric \ntonnes)ȣ\nFY 2025-26FY 2024-25\nCategory of waste:\n(i)  Incineration0.050.07\n(ii)  Landfilling0.000.00\n(iii)  Ot\nher disposal operations 0.001.53\nTo t a l0.051.60\n    *Environmental performance data for this indicator covers three offices on direct lease (Gurugram, Bengaluru and Mumbai). \nDisclosure in different waste categories is made as per Eternal’s boundary determined by operational control approach. Please \nrefer to our boundary note on page 86 of this annual report for more details.\n                  Note: Indicate if any independent assessment/ evaluation/ assurance has  been carried out by an external \nagency? (Y/ N) If yes, name of the external agency - \n Yes, assured by Deloitte Haskins & Sells LLP for FY26. \n10.  BrieŦy describe the waste management practices adopted in your establishments. Describe \nthe strategy adopted by your company to reduce the usage of hazardous and toxic chemicals \nin your products and processes and the practices adopted to manage such wastes.\n    Eternal Limited (formerly known as Zomato Limited) is a technology platform services provider. Our core \nbusiness  processes do not result in the generation of waste beyond office waste within which no toxic \nwaste is generated and hazardous waste is minimal.\n    However, we are deeply committed to effective waste management strategies to minimize environmental \nimpacts of waste in our offices and in our value chain.\n Office waste management\n    Our employees sit out of leased premises or co-working spaces where waste disposal is managed by the \nowners or main lessors of the property. In offices that are directly leased by us, we have implemented \ninitiatives to reduce waste and promote segregation at the source. \n    We promote the use of sustainable alternatives within our workplaces through provision of reusable \ncutlery and plates, implementation of a ‘Bring Your Own Bottle (BYOB)’ campaign, use of printing paper \nmade from wheat straw (agri-residue). Our office coffee mugs are biodegradable and made of fallen \npine needles, thereby reducing reliance on single-use items and virgin resources.\n    We utilize color coded and labelled bins to ensure segregation of waste streams, supported by \ndedicated housekeeping teams. For e-waste, biomedical and hazardous waste, we appoint authorized \nvendors and obtain disposal and recycling certificates. For other waste streams such as plastic, wet \nwaste and paper and cardboard, that are managed by landlords or facility operators, we obtain details \non disposal and recycling from their appointed vendors.\n    We conduct regular awareness campaigns and training initiatives on responsible consumption \nand disposal habits among our employees. These efforts are aimed at promoting efficient waste \nsegregation at source.  \n    We \ncontinue to invest in innovative and sustainable waste management practices. For example, \nin FY26, we onboarded Padcare as our menstrual waste management vendor across our offices. \nPadcare collects used sanitary pads and recycles them into hygienic pulp and plastic, offering a safer \nalternative to landfilling and incineration. \n    In l\nine with our environmental best practices, our Gurugram and Bengaluru offices hold ISO 14001 \ncertification, demonstrating our commitment to effective environmental management. \n\n140\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\n Value chain waste management\n    Packaging and food waste are the two kinds of waste associated with food delivery orders sent by \nrestaurants to end-consumers via the Zomato platform. Restaurants choose the kind of packaging they \nwish to use to deliver food. Restaurant practices also determine the amount of waste generated in the \ncooking process. End-consumers control the number of orders they may place, cancellation decisions and \nprocess of disposal of packaging and leftovers (after consuming food). We run several voluntary initiatives \nto positively influence behaviours of both stakeholders with respect to waste management. \n  Reducing food and cutlery waste\n                     Th\ne Zomato app offers consumers the option of making special requests at the time of ordering, \nincluding a request to the restaurant not to send cutlery, thereby reducing cutlery waste. Between \nFY22 and FY26, this initiative has helped avoid over 3,000 MT of cutlery waste.  \n                     Ad\nditionally, The ‘Food Rescue’ initiative further addresses food wastage by making canceled \norders available for nearby customers in their original untampered packaging at discounted \nprices. Since launch in FY25, Zomato’s Food Rescue feature has helped prevent over 7.5 lakh kg \nof food waste by diverting cancelled orders to interested buyers (assuming each order weighs \napproximately 400g).  \n   Rewarding adopters and innovators of sustainable packaging \n                     We \nintroduced the Zomato Plastic-Free Future Program in December 2024 for restaurant partners \nactively adopting sustainable alternatives to plastic packaging for food deliveries. To recognize \ntheir efforts, these restaurants now carry a prominent ‘Low plastic packaging’ tag on their menu \npages, banners and a clear label in the order summary, making it easy for customers to identify \nthese restaurants. Customers can also discover these restaurants faster by typing ‘Low plastic \npackaging’ in the search bar or using the ‘Low plastic packaging’ filter option. As of March 31\nst\n \n2026, over 100 million ‘Low plastic packaging’ orders had been delivered from restaurants that \nhad signed up to the Plastic-Free Future Program. \n                     To promote innovation on alternatives to plastic packaging for the food delivery use-case, we \nrun an annual event on sustainable packaging bringing start-up innovators and restaurateurs to \nthe same platform. In FY26, Zomato Plastic-Free Future Packathon was held in partnership with \nStartup India and Hyperpure by Zomato attracting applications from more than 125 sustainable \npackaging start-ups all over India. \n  Voluntar\ny recycling\n                     Plastic continues to play an important role in packaging for food deliveries for a few important \nreasons. Many restaurants, especially those that offer Indian and Oriental cuisine, choose plastic \ncontainers, trays and bottles for food deliveries on account of lack of suitable sustainable \nalternatives for liquids and ‘wet’ cuisine such as  gravies, soups and beverages. Apart from \nfunctional reasons, plastic is relatively affordable and more available across India. In order to \noffset the impact of plastic packaging used by restaurants on the environment, in FY23, we \nlaunched a voluntary plastic waste recycling initiative called the 100% plastic neutral deliveries \ninitiative. Since launch, Eternal has voluntarily recycled 60,000 MT of plastic waste from FY23- \nFY26 (15,000 MT was recycled in FY26 alone) fully meeting the target of 100% plastic neutral \ndeliveries as per independent estimates.\n\n1 41\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\n11.   If the entity has operations/ offices in/ around ecologically sensitive areas (such as national \nparks, wildlife sanctuaries, biosphere reserves, wetlands, biodiversity hotspots, forests, coastal \nregulation zones, etc.) where environmental approvals/ clearances are required, please specify \ndetails in the following format:\n Eternal does not have any offices in/ around ecologically sensitive areas.\n12.   Details of Environmental Impact Assessments of projects undertaken by the entity based on \napplicable laws, in the current financial year: \n Not applicable.\n13.    Is the entity compliant with the applicable environmental law/ regulations/ guidelines in India; \nsuch  as  the  Water  (prevention  and  control  of  pollution)  Act,  Air  (prevention  and  control  of  \npollution) Act, Environment Protection Act, and rules thereunder (Y/ N). If not, provide details \nof all such non-compliances, in the following format:\n Yes.\nLeadership Indicators\n2.  Please provide details of total Scope 3 emissions & its intensity, in the following format:\nParameterUnitF 2025-26ȣFY 2024-25\nTotal Scope 3 emissions (Break-up of the \nGHG into CO\n2\n, CH4, N2O, HFCs, PFCs, SF6, \nNF3, if available)\nMetric tonnes of \nCO\n2\n equivalent\n1,47,101.39 tCO\n2\ne1,32,089.34 tCO\n2\ne\nTotal Scope 3 emissions per rupee of \nturnover\nMetric tonnes of \nCO\n2\n equivalent\n0.0000013496779\ntCO\n2\ne/ Rupee of \nturnover\n0.0000015328924\ntCO\n2\ne/ Rupee of \nturnover\nTotal Scope 3 emission intensity (optional) – the \nrelevant metric may be selected by the entity\n--\n    *Scope 3 emissions are calculated using emission factors sourced from the India GHG Program, the UK Department for Environment, \nFood & Rural Affairs (DEFRA), USEPA- EEIO Emission factors Version 1.3, and the Central Electricity Authority (CEA) 2025 emission \nfactors.\n  Note: Indicate if any independent assessment/ evaluation/ assurance has been carried out by an external \nagency? (Y/ N) If yes, name of the external agency. \n Ye\ns, assured by Deloitte Haskins & Sells LLP for FY26.  \n\n142\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\n4.    If the entity has undertaken any specific initiatives or used innovative technology or solutions \nto improve resource efficiency, or reduce impact due to emissions / eũuent discharge/waste \ngenerated, please provide details of the same as well as the outcome of such initiatives, as per \nthe following format: \n    Eternal Limited (formerly known as Zomato Limited) is a technology platform services provider and \ndoes not produce any physical products. Eternal has minimal environmental impacts through its direct \noperations. The initiatives below describe voluntary initiatives taken within our value chain featured under \n‘Climate action and circular economy’ material topic to minimize environmental impacts across our value \nchain - both upstream (suppliers) and downstream (customers):\nS. NoInitiative \nundertaken \nDetails of the initiative (Web-link, if any, may be \nprovided along-with summary)\nOutcome of the initiative \n1.Transition to \nEV-based food \ndeliveries\nOur key climate mitigation action is to support \ndelivery partners in transitioning to EV bikes \nto undertake food deliveries. Towards this \ngoal, we invest in creating awareness about \nEVs among delivery partners, partner with EV \necosystem players to secure their access to EVs \non rent & and advocate for better EV products, \ninfrastructure and financing.\nEV awareness: \n\n              Com\nmunication channels- We release \nengaging videos on our ‘Zomato Delivery \nPartners’ youtube channel to generate \nawareness among delivery partners about \nthe benefits of using EVs for deliveries. \nThis channel is very popular among delivery \npartners. We also have a communication \nhub on Zomato’s delivery partner app that \nupdates partners on initiatives that can be \nbeneficial, including new EV partnerships, \nfinancial products and offers for owning an \nEV bike. \n\n              EV Bazaar- We bridge the gaps between \ndelivery partners and EV ecosystem \nproviders, through on-ground events like EV \nmelas and bazaars. In September 2025, we \norganized the second edition of EV Bazaar in \nBengaluru.\nEV awareness: \n\n              Com\nmunication channels- In FY26, \ndelivery partners were made aware of the \nbenefits of transitioning to EVs through \nmultiple engaging videos on the YouTube \nchannel, EV campaigns launched within \nthe Zomato delivery partner app and \nalso through notifications. A total of 20 \nEV-related awareness videos have been \nreleased for delivery partners resulting in \nover 4,00,000 views. In FY26, we launched \nfive EV campaigns, engaging 10 lakh+ \ndelivery partners and generated over 96 \nlakh impressions.\n\n              EV B\nazaar- The second edition of EV \nbazaar event in Bengaluru was attended \nby more than 1,000 delivery partners and \nmore than 20 ecosystem providers which \nhelped enhance their awareness about the \nEV ecosystem. Since its inception, the EV \nBazaar initiative has achieved significant \noutreach, engaging over 2,000 delivery \npartners and more than 20 ecosystem \nproviders across Delhi and Bengaluru.\n\n143\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nS. NoInitiative \nundertaken \nDetails of the initiative (Web-link, if any, may be \nprovided along-with summary)\nOutcome of the initiative \nTransition to \nEV-based food \ndeliveries\nEV access (via partnerships): \n\n   We established partnership with EV rental, \nfinancing, swapping and logistics companies \nto help in facilitating seamless access to EVs \non rent. \n\n   We launched the Zomato EV rental fleet \nacross Delhi and Chennai to make electric \nvehicles more accessible for our delivery \npartners.\nEV advocacy: \n\n   We continuously advocate for better EV \nproducts, infrastructure and financing for \ndelivery partners. We are a part of global \ncoalitions on EVs including the Climate \nGroup’s EV100 initiative and the UNEP \nsupported Deliver-e Coalition. We regularly \ncontribute to stakeholder consultations \nfacilitated by central, state and city-level \ngovernment on the topic of EVs.\nEV access (via partnerships): \n\n    We collaborate with EV ecosystem players \nto secure access for delivery partners to \nEVs on rent and advocate for better EV \nproducts, infrastructure and financing. \nIt enabled delivery partners to view and \nbook EVs on rent through the ‘Rent an EV’ \nfeature on their app. They are also able \nto view conveniently located charging \nand swapping points that fall in their zone \ndirectly on the delivery partner app. 23,675 \ndelivery partners have rented EVs via the \n“Rent an EV” feature in FY26, totaling to \n48,686 rentals since its launch in FY25. \n\n    As of March 2026, more than 1,500 EVs \nhave been deployed across Delhi and \nChennai under the Zomato EV rental fleet \ninitiative.  \nEV advocacy:\n\n    Through participation in global EV \ncoalitions such as Climate Group’s EV100 \nand the UNEP supported Deliver-E \nCoalition, the company enabled cross-\nindustry collaboration, shared best \npractices, and contributed to scalable \nsolutions for key EV adoption challenges \nacross last-mile logistics, including \ncharging infrastructure, financing, and \noperational accessibility. Engagement with \ngovernment stakeholders at central and \nstate levels also supported policy dialogue \nfor strengthening the EV ecosystem in last-\nmile logistics.\nOur above initiatives across EV awareness, \naccess and advocacy to support delivery \npartners in transitioning to electric bikes for \nundertaking food deliveries have led to the \nfollowing outcomes:\n\n   \n \nOu\nr monthly active EV-based delivery fleet \nas on March 2026 stood at 63,435 which is \n~70% increase since March 2025. \n\n   \n \n13\n.5% of our food delivery orders were \ndelivered by EVs in FY26, helping avoid \n7,304 tCO2e (scope 3) emissions. \n\n   \n \nIn F\nY26, our last-mile delivery emissions \non a per km basis reduced by 11.77 % \ncompared to FY22. \n2.Climate \nadaptation \nsupport for \ndelivery \npartners\nAutomatic weather stations: \n\n   We have been building a proprietary network \nof onground weather stations that provides \nprecise and real-time data on key weather \nparameters such as temperature, wind \nspeed, rainfall, etc. This initiative supports \ninformed business decisions to better serve \nour customers and delivery partners under \nvarious climatic conditions.\nAutomatic weather stations:\n\n   I\nn FY26, we expanded our Automated \nWeather Stations (AWS) network to 1,150 \ndevices across 100 cities to guide business \ndecisions and to predict and communicate \nweather conditions to our delivery \npartners. \n\n144\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nS. NoInitiative \nundertaken \nDetails of the initiative (Web-link, if any, may be \nprovided along-with summary)\nOutcome of the initiative \n3.Voluntary \nrecycling\n              In FY23, Zomato app launched the 100% \nPlastic Neutral Deliveries initiative. While \nthe choice of packaging used for food \ndeliveries rests with our restaurant partners, \nwe are committed to mitigating the impact \nof this packaging on the environment \nthrough voluntary recycling. To estimate the \nvolume of plastic used by restaurants for \npackaging, we engaged an expert third party \nto review data from orders by dish type, \nrestaurant location and price of meals. This \ndata was supplemented by an independent \nsurvey of the packaging practices of over \n600 restaurants. Zomato then engaged \nauthorized waste management agencies to \nundertake recycling of plastic waste equal \nto the volume estimated by the third party \nstudy of restaurant packaging. \n Refer to this link for further details\n\n              Si\nnce launch, Eternal has voluntarily \nrecycled 60,000 MT of plastic waste from \nFY23- FY26 (15,000 MT was recycled in \nFY26 alone) fully meeting the target of \n100% plastic neutral deliveries as per \nindependent estimates.\n4.Rewarding \nadopters and \ninnovators of \nsustainable \npackaging \n   Plastic-Free Future Program: Restaurant \npartners choose and procure their own \npackaging for food delivery based on cuisine \ntype, brand and budget. We introduced \nthe Zomato Plastic-Free Future Program \nin December 2024 to recognise restaurant \npartners actively adopting alternatives to \nplastic packaging for food deliveries.\n                  To r\necognize their efforts, these \nrestaurants now carry a prominent ‘low \nplastic packaging’ tag on their menu pages, \nbanners, and a clear label in the order \nsummary, making it easy for customers to \nidentify these restaurants. Customers can \nalso discover these restaurants faster by \ntyping ‘low plastic packaging’ in the search \nbar or by using the ‘low plastic packaging’ \nfilter option. \n                  Our aim is to motivate more restaurants to \nmake the switch from plastic to sustainable \npackaging alternatives while making it \neasier for environment-conscious and/or \npackaging-conscious customers to discover \nand order from these restaurants easily. A \nsurge in customer awareness and usage is \nlikely to drive greater adoption of sustainable \npackaging among restaurants. \n (Li\nnk:  Plastic-Free Future Program) \n   P\nlastic-Free Future Program: More than \n350 restaurant brands, representing \n10,000+ restaurants across 500+ cities \nlisted on the Zomato app have already \njoined the program by submitting detailed \nevidence of their packaging, including \nphotos and certificates, confirming their \nmain menu items are being packed in \nsustainable alternatives. As of March \n31\nst\n 2026, over 100 million ‘low plastic \npackaging’ orders had been delivered \nfrom restaurants that had signed up to the \nPlastic-Free Future Program. \n\n145\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nS. NoInitiative \nundertaken \nDetails of the initiative (Web-link, if any, may be \nprovided along-with summary)\nOutcome of the initiative \nRewarding \nadopters and \ninnovators of \nsustainable \npackaging\n\n  Plastic Free Future Packathon: While \nrestaurants choose their own packaging, \nalternatives to plastic packaging are limited \nin the market. To promote innovation \non alternatives to plastic packaging for \nthe food delivery use-case, we run the \nZomato Plastic-Free Future Packathon \nin partnership with Startup India and \nHyperpure by Zomato which serves over \n2.6 lakh+ restaurants. This annual contest \nsurfaces and spotlights startups working on \ninnovative sustainable packaging solutions \nfor food delivery.\n\n   Pl\nastic Free Future Packathon: The \nsecond edition of the Packathon \nrecognized two winners across two \ncategories - ready-to-scale and prototype \n- from 128 applicants from 21 states. 47 \nstart-ups were led by women.\n    Re\nady-to-scale startup winners Imaga \nand Go Do Good showcased bamboo \nfiber-based food delivery packaging that \ncan effectively pack oily food items, and \nplant-based barrier coatings that are oil \nand grease resistant respectively. In the \nprototype category, Agrileaf Exports \npresented takeaway containers made \nof areca palm leaves with heat-sealable \nlids, and Earthworks Biomat showcased \ndeliveryware made from natural fibers and \nbiopolymers derived from agri-waste.\n    Additionally, we presented the first Zomato \nPlastic-Free Packaging Awards to 24 \nnational and regional restaurant brands that \nhave successfully shifted to sustainable \npackaging alternatives while ensuring a high-\nquality customer experience.\n    Ac\nross the two editions of the Zomato \nPackathon, many exciting innovative \nsolutions have been highlighted, including \nedible cutlery, barrier coatings made of \nplant-based gums and seaweed and patented \ncarry bags made of agri-waste. These \ninnovations were displayed to all attendees \nthrough an extensive exhibition featuring \n41 enterprises working on plastic-free \npackaging solutions for food delivery. In \nFY26, Zomato Plastic-Free Future Packathon \nwas held in partnership with Startup India \nand Hyperpure by Zomato attracting \napplications from more than 125 sustainable \npackaging start-ups all over India.\n    The Zomato Plastic-Free Future Program \n(and the Zomato Packathon) is a one-of-a-\nkind initiative by a food delivery platform \nglobally to actively encourage the restaurant \nindustry to adopt sustainable alternatives \nwhile working on unlocking progress for the \nIndian sustainable packaging ecosystem.\n5.Reducing food \nand cutlery \nwaste\n\n  Re\nducing cutlery waste: The Zomato app \noffers consumers the option of making \nspecial requests at the time of ordering, \nincluding a request to the restaurant not to \nsend cutlery, thereby reducing cutlery waste.\n\n  Foo\nd rescue initiative: Food wastage is a \nsignificant challenge, given that every month, \naround 4,00,000 perfectly good orders already \non their way to customers are canceled on the \nZomato app. To reduce this food wastage, we \nintroduced ‘Food Rescue’, an initiative that \nmakes canceled orders available for nearby \ncustomers in their original untampered \npackaging at unbeatable prices within minutes. \n\n  Re\nducing cutlery waste: Between FY22 \nand FY26, this initiative has helped avoid \nover 3,000 MT of cutlery waste in total.  \n  Food rescue initiative: Since launch in \nFY25, Zomato’s Food Rescue feature has \nhelped prevent over 7.5 lakh kg of food \nwaste by diverting cancelled orders to \ninterested buyers (assuming each order \nweighs approximately 400g). \n\n146\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\n5.     Does the entity have a business continuity and disaster management plan? Give details in 100 \nwords/ web link.\n    Yes. Eternal Limited has established a Business Continuity Management (BCM) Program, which includes \nformal Business Continuity and Crisis Management plans. The framework is designed to proactively \nidentify threats and mitigate risks associated with potential business disruptions (including climate). Our \nprogram includes regular Risk Assessments and Business Impact Analysis (BIA) to evaluate and manage \nfinancial, operational and reputational consequences. The plans define clear recovery strategies to ensure \nthe swift restoration of critical business operations (including technology systems). Furthermore, we \nmaintain organizational readiness through periodic testing, training and oversight by a dedicated team.\n6.    Disclose any significant adverse impact to the environment, arising from the value chain of  the \nentity. What mitigation or adaptation measures have been taken by the entity in this regard.\n    Eternal’s value chain does not have any adverse impact on the environment. We proactively help our value \nchain partners in reducing their environmental impact through collaborative efforts. These initiatives have \nbeen described in the above table (Principle 6, Leadership indicator 4).\n7.      Percentage of value chain partners (by value of business done with such partners) that were \nassessed for environmental impacts.\n% of value chain partners \n (by value of business done with such partners) that were assessedȣ\nEnvironmental impacts   16.95% by total spend on suppliers (upstream)**\n5.08% by total revenue from restaurant partners (downstream)***\n *Value chain disclosures are based on the previous financial year (FY25).\n    **The assessment was conducted for 50 upstream suppliers. During the year, we significantly expanded the coverage of supplier \nassessment compared to the previous year. While the percentage based on spend reflects changes in procurement mix and supplier \nspend distribution within the year, the program now covers a broader set of value chain partners.\n **\n*The assessment was conducted for 8 downstream customers.\nPrinciple 7 – Businesses, when engaging in inŦuencing public and regulatory policy, should do so in a \nmanner that is responsible and transparent\nEssential Indicators\n1.  a.  Number of affiliations with trade and industry chambers/ associations.\n       Eternal Limited (formerly known as Zomato Limited) was affiliated with/ participated in discussions \nwith six industry bodies.\n b.     List the top 10 trade and industry chambers/ associations (determined based on the total \nmembers of such a body) the entity is a member of/ affiliated to.\nS. \nNo.\nName of the trade and industry chambers/ \nassociations\nReach of trade and industry chambers/ \nassociations (State/ National) \n1.  Internet and Mobile Association of India (IAMAI)National\n2. Federation of Indian Chambers of Commerce and \nIndustry (FICCI)\nNational\n3.\nTechnology Services Industry Association \n(Indiatech.org)\nNational\n4.  National Association of Software and Service \nCompanies (NASSCOM)\nNational\n5.  Confederation of Indian Industry (CII)National\n6.  PHD Chamber of Commerce and Industry \n(PHDCCI)*\nNational\n    *Membership existed until December 2025\n\n1 47\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\n2.     Provide details of corrective action taken or underway on any issues related to anti-competitive \nconduct by the entity, based on adverse orders from regulatory authorities.\nName of authorityBrief of the caseCorrective action taken\nNone. No adverse order received by the Company from regulatory authorities in FY26*.\n    *During the year ended March 31, 2022, the Company received an order under Section 26(1) of the Competition Act, 2002, under \nwhich the Hon’ble Competition Commission of India (CCI) initiated an investigation into certain aspects of the Company’s business. \nThe Company continues to work closely with the Hon’ble CCI to assist them with their inquiry and explain to the Hon’ble CCI why all \nits practices are in compliance with competition laws and do not have any adverse effect on competition in India. \nLeadership Indicators\n1.  Details of public policy positions advocated by the entity:\nS. \nNo.\nPublic policy \nadvocated \nMethod resorted for such \nadvocacy\nWhether \ninformation \navailable in \nthe public \ndomain? \n(Yes/ No)\nFrequency of \nreview by board \n(Annually/ half \nyearly/ quarterly/ \nothers – please \nspecif y) \nWeb-link, if available\n1.   Provided inputs on \nDirection No. 94 of \nthe Commission \nfor Air Quality \nManagement \n(CAQM) \nEternal, along with other \nindustry stakeholders, \nparticipated in \nconsultations on Direction \nNo. 94 issued by the CAQM.\nYes-\nhttps://www.pib.gov.\nin/PressReleasePage.\naspx?PRID=2207584&reg=3&lang=2\n2. Provided inputs on \ndraft rules under \n