ith integrity, \nand in a manner that is Ethical, \nTransparent and Accountable \nE-1: Percentage coverage by training and awareness \nprogrammes on any of the Principles during the Financial Year\n3   P-3: Businesses should respect \nand promote the wellbeing of all \nemployees, including those in their \nvalue chains \nE-5: Return to work and Retention rates of permanent \nemployees and workers that took parental leave. \nE-9: Details of performance and career development reviews \nof employees and worker\n4   P-5: Businesses should respect \nand promote human rights \nE-1: Number of Employees and workers who have been provided \ntraining on human rights issues and policies of the entity.\nE-3: Details of remuneration/salary/wages.  \nNote:\n1.  Indicators assured under Principle 6 are reported for leased offices under operational control of the Company.\n\n160\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: BRSR\nCompany Overview\nAPPENDIX II\nIdentified Sustainability Information subject to Limited Assurance \nPart A: BRSR Indicators (Other than BRSR Core)\nS r. \nNo\nReporting Standard ReferenceDescription of Indicator\n1  Section A: General disclosures   E-20a: Employees and workers (including differently abled) \nE-20b: Differently abled Employees and workers.  \nE-25: Complaints/Grievances on any of the principles (Principles 1 to \n9) under the National Guidelines on Responsible Business Conduct \nSection C - Principle (P) wise Performance Disclosure- Essential Indicator [E] / Leadership Indicator [L]\n2  P-1: Businesses should conduct \nand govern themselves with \nintegrity, and in a manner that \nis Ethical, Transparent and \nAccountable\nE-6: Details of complaints with regard to conflict of interest \n3  P-3: Businesses should respect \nand promote the wellbeing of all \nemployees, including those in \ntheir value chains\nE-1a: Details of measures for the wellbeing of employees: (Health \ninsurance, Accident insurance, Maternity Benefits, Day Care \nfacilities) \nE-1b: Details of measures for the wellbeing of workers: (Health \ninsurance, Accident insurance, Maternity Benefits, Day Care \nfacilities) \nE-2: Details of retirement benefits, for Current Financial Year \n(excluding amounts deducted and deposited with the authority) \nE-7: Membership of employees and worker in association(s) or Unions \nrecognised by the listed entity \nE-13: Number of Complaints on working conditions & Health safety \nmade by employees and workers \nE-14: Assessments for the year (Health and safety practices, Working \nConditions) \n4  P-5: Businesses should respect \nand promote human rights\nE-2: Details of minimum wages paid to employees and workers. \nE-6: Number of Complaints on (Sexual Harassment, Discrimination \nat workplace, Child Labour, Forced Labour/Involuntary Labour, \nWages and Other human rights related issues) made by employees \nand workers. \nE-10: Percentage of your plants and offices that were assessed \n(by entity or statutory authorities or third parties) on Sexual \nHarassment, Discrimination at workplace, Child Labour, Forced \nLabour/Involuntary Labour, Wages and Other human rights related \nissues \n5  P-6: Businesses should respect \nand make efforts to protect and \nrestore the environment. (Refer \nNote 1) \nL-2: Details of total Scope 3 emissions & its intensity \n6  P-9: Businesses should engage \nwith and provide value to their \nconsumers in a responsible \nmanner \nE-3: Number of consumer complaints in respect of Data Privacy, \nAdvertising,  Cyber-Security,  Delivery  of  essential  services, \nRestrictive Trade Practices, Unfair Trade Practices  \nNote:\n1.\n In\ndicators under Principle 6 are reported for leased offices under operational control of the Company.\n\nReport on Corporate Governance (CGR)\n1.     Company’s     philosophy     on     \nCorporate Governance\nOur  corporate  governance  philosophy  is  rooted  \nin   integrity,   transparency,   accountability,   and   \nresponsible business conduct, ensuring the protection \nof stakeholder interests and driving long-term value \ncreation. This commitment to ethical and sustainable \npractices shapes our organizational decision-making \nand drives compliance with applicable laws in line with \nthe charter documents of the Company in both letter \nand spirit. At the core of our governance framework \nis the strategic oversight and independent judgement \nprovided  by  our  Board,  who  guide  the  Company’s  \naffairs  and  supervise  the  leadership  team’s  day-to-\nday  operations  through  specialized  committees.  \nTo  further  foster  a  robust  culture  of  accountability,  \nwe   have   implemented   a   comprehensive   policy   \nframework that includes specific codes of conduct \nfor  employees,  senior  management,  and  the  Board,  \nalongside strict guidelines for business partners and \nfor prevention of insider trading.\n2. Board of Directors\nThe  Board  steers  the  strategy,  performance,  and  \ngovernance,   establishing   a   robust   operational   \nframework  to  drive  sustainable,  long-term  value  for  \nall  stakeholders.  The  Board  maintains  independent  \noversight   to   ensure   management   consistently   \ndelivers  on  its  commitments.  The  directors  bring  \ntogether  a  diverse  mix  of  specialized  skills  and  deep  \nindustry experience with strong leadership to execute \nits responsibilities with high efficiency.\nThe Board has 7 (Seven) committees comprising of \nBoard  members  to  discharge  the  responsibilities  \nassigned to them.\nComposition and inter-se relationship\nDuring the financial year under review, the composition \nof the Board was in conformity with Regulation 17 of \nthe SEBI Listing Regulations and Section 149 and 152 \nof  the  Act.  The  Company  operates  with  a  one-tier  \nsystem  featuring  an  optimum  mix  of  non-executive  \ndirectors including women directors. \nThe Board comprises 6 (six) directors of whom 4 (four) \nare independent directors (including 3 (three) women \nindependent directors) and 2 (two) non-executive \ndirectors, (including 1 (one) nominee director). None \nof the directors share any inter-se relationships, each \none of them is independent of the other, and all non-\nexecutive directors are under the age of seventy-five \nyears.\nThe detailed profiles of our directors and individual \ncommittee compositions can be accessed at Link.\nBoard Meetings\nOur  Board  meets  at  regular  intervals  to  deliberate  \nand  decide  on  operations,  key  strategic  issues,  and  \nstatutory matters. To maintain consistent momentum, \nthe  gap  between  two  consecutive  meetings  never  \nexceeds 120 days. The notice, detailed agenda \ntogether  with  relevant  notes  and  other  material  \ninformation  are  generally  shared  with  the  directors  \nsufficiently in advance of Board meetings, enabling \neffective   deliberations   and   informed   decision-\nmaking.\nDuring the financial year under review, the Board met \n8 (eight) times and all the directors of the Company \nattended  at  least  one  board  meeting  during  such  \nperiod.  Further,  the  details  of  the  Board  meetings,  \nAGM held during the financial year and directors’ \nattendance thereat, are provided below:\n161\nFinancial StatementsStatutory Reports: CGR\nCompany Overview\n\nName of \ndirector\n Board meeting (s) AGM\nApril 18, \n2025\nMay 1, \n2025\nJuly 21, \n2025\nOctober \n16, 2025\nJanuary \n21, 2026\n1\nFebruar y \n10, 2026\nMarch \n12, 2026\nBoard Meeting \nattendance in (%) \nAugust \n19, 2025\nAparna Popat \nVed\n100\nDeepinder \nGoyal\n2\n100\nKaushik Dutta100\nNamita Gupta100\nSanjeev \nBikhchandani\n100\nSutapa \nBanerjee\n100\n1\nTwo Board meetings were held on January 21, 2026 and were attended by all directors, therefore, these two meetings are shown in a \nsingle column in the above table.\n2\nDeepinder Goyal resigned as Director, Managing Director & Chief Executive Officer of the Comp