00\n20% off\nGra\nnd\n Bell \nK\nitche\nn\n30\n-\n3\n5\n mins\nO\nr\nde\nr \nagain\nCa\nny\no\nn\n \nR\nistora\nn\nte\n30 mins\n4\n0% off up to $100\n2\n5\n% off\nAv\ni\nd\n Bell Bistro\n30\n-\n3\n5\n mins\nsee all\nY\no\nu\nr \nli\nk\nes\nli\nk\ne\nd\n 3 \nd\nishes\nCa\nny\no\nn\n \nR\nistora\nn\nte\nli\nk\ne\nd\n \nr\nestau\nr\nant\nAv\ni\nd\n Bell Bistro\nGarlic Cup Bistro\nli\nk\ne\nd\n 1 \nd\nish\nSor\nt\nL\ni\nk\ne\nd\nR\nate\nd\n 4.0\n+\nOff\ners\nD\neli\nv\ner\ny\n time\n”aq“Wd\n \nBist\nro\n4.9\nGet 40\n%\n o\nff\n up to \n₹\n100\np“q\nr\nbuc\nk\ns\n4.9\nGet 40\n%\n o\nff\n up to \n₹\n100\np“q\nr\nbuc\nk\ns\n4.9\nGet 40\n%\n o\nff\n up to \n₹\n100\nE\nn\ng\nlish\n9\neli\nv\ner\ny\n9\ni\nn\ni\nn\ng\n100\ner\n5\no\nn\ne\ny\nCompany Overview: Operational and financial highlights  |  Statutory Reports  |  Financial Statements\ni. Food delivery\n\nOur average monthly active food delivery restaurant partner base \nincreased by 64% to 180k in FY22. We ended this fiscal year with \n205k average monthly active food delivery restaurant partners in Q4 \nFY22. We continued to support several restaurants as they \nrecovered from the impact of Covid.\nGrowth in supply of restaurants on our platform\nExpansion of our delivery partner network\nOur average monthly active delivery partner base grew by 139% to \n285k in FY22. We ended this fiscal year with 316k average monthly \nactive delivery partners in Q4 FY22 to support the increase in order \nvolumes. We provide riders with attractive and flexible earning \nopportunities. They are also provided with free accidental & \nmedical insurance cover along with disability / death coverage. We \nhave also instituted a variety of programs for the overall welfare of \nour delivery partners and their families.\n61\nFY19\n131\nFY20\n110\nFY21\n180\nFY22\nAverage monthly active food delivery restaurant partners\n‘000\n81\nFY19\n189\nFY20\n120\nFY21\n285\nFY22\nAverage monthly active delivery partners\n‘000\n7\nCompany Overview: Operational and financial highlights  |  Statutory Reports  |  Financial Statements\n\nWe expanded our food delivery offering to over \n1,000+ locations by the end of this fiscal year (up \nfrom 455 cities as at the end of FY21). While the \nGOV contribution from these new markets is very \nlow currently, we have seeded these markets early \nwith the objective of being a first mover and \nbenefiting from growth as these markets scale \nover time. Historically, we have seen markets \nbeyond the top 8 cities become significant \ncontributors to our overall GOV over time.\nFootprint in India doubled from 455 cities to \n1,000+ cities\nFood delivery active cities\n#\nFood delivery GOV\nINR billion\n203\nMar’19\n490\nMar’20\n455\nMar’21\n1,000+\nMar’22FY19\n53.9\n112.2\nFY20\n94.8\nFY21\n213.0\nFY22\nTop 8 cities GOVOthers cities GOV\n8\nCompany Overview: Operational and financial highlights  |  Statutory Reports  |  Financial Statements\n\nContribution as a % of GOV was 1.7% in \nFY22 compared to 5.2% in FY21. The fall \nin contribution margin is attributable \nprimarily to increase in last-mile delivery \ncosts as well as investments to drive \ncustomer acquisition and retention with \na view to support future growth. \nContribution remained positive in FY22, \nalthough contribution margin declined \nvis-a-vis FY21\nFood delivery contribution margin\n% of GOV\n-24.8%\nFY19\n-11.0%\nFY20\n5.2%\nFY21\n1.7%\nFY22\n9\nCompany Overview: Operational and financial highlights  |  Statutory Reports  |  Financial Statements\nFood delivery Adjusted EBITDA as a % \nof GOV was -1.8% in FY22 compared to \n0.4% in FY21 driven by decrease in \ncontribution margin as explained \nabove.\nAdjusted EBITDA declined marginally \nto -1.8% in FY22\nFood delivery Adjusted EBITDA margin\n% of GOV\n-37.0%\nFY19\n-17.2%\nFY20\n-1.8%\nFY22\n0.4%\nFY21\nGOV reached INR 213.0 billion in FY22, a \nyear-on-year (“YoY”) increase of 125% \nover FY21. This was driven by healthy \ngrowth in order volumes while the \naverage order value remained stable.\n\n\nAdjusted Revenue has largely increased \nin line with increase in GOV in FY22.\nGOV grew to a record high this year\nFood delivery GOV\nINR billion\n53.9\nFY19\n9.1\n112.2\nFY20\n23.6\n94.8\nFY21\n21.6\n213.0\nFY22\n47.6\nGOVFood delivery Adjusted Revenue\n\nIn FY22, Hyperpure expanded its presence to 10 cities across India through 20 warehouses and provided supplies \nto 51k unique restaurants. \nHyperpure revenues increased by 170% in FY22 to INR 5.4 billion primarily driven by addition of new \ncustomers and expansion into new cities. Adjusted EBITDA margin remained flattish at -22% in FY22 vis-a-\nvis -23% in FY21 due to investments in city expansion. \n10\nHyperpure active cities\n#\n1\nFY19\n2\nFY20\n6\nFY21\n10\nFY22\nHyperpure revenue\nINR billion\n0.1\nFY19\n1.1\nFY20\n2.0\nFY21\n5.4\nFY22\nHyperpure Adjusted EBITDA margin\n% of Adjusted Revenue\n-75%\nFY19\n-46%\nFY20\n-23%\nFY21\n-22%\nFY22\nHyperpure unique restaurant customer base\n‘000\nFY19\n4\nFY20\n25\nFY21\n51\nFY22\n1\nCompany Overview: Operational & financial highlights  |  Statutory Reports  |  Financial Statements\nii. Hyperpure\n\n11\nRevenue decreased 15% YoY in FY22, primarily on account of shut down of most of our international \noperations as we decided to focus only on the India business. The Company also discontinued other non-core \nbusinesses during the year.\n\n\nDining-out and membership businesses have been adversely impacted in FY21 and FY22 primarily on account \nof Covid related lockdowns. \niii. Dining-out and others\nCompany Overview: Operational & financial highlights  |  Statutory Reports  |  Financial Statements\nRevenue\nINR billion\n4.8\nFY19\n6.6\nFY20\n2.8\nFY21\n2.4\nFY22\nAdjusted EBITDA \nINR billion\n1.0\nFY19\n1.3\nFY20\n-0.6\nFY21\n-0.2\nFY22\n\nFY22 operational and financial highlights summary\n12\nCompany Overview: Operational & financial highlights  |  Statutory Reports  |  Financial Statements\nGOV\nINR billion, 